WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.650
- In this Opening Faculty Roundtable,

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.440
entitled "Race and the
Transformation of Disciplines,"

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.560
we are extremely fortunate to
have five terrific scholars

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.490
who will share their
perspectives on these issues.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.010
Each will offer approximately five minutes

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.540
of opening comments,
which will be followed

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.870
by a brief intra-panel discussion,

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.220
and then we will have
the remainder of our time

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.320
to take your questions.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.980
I'm going to now offer a
very brief introduction

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.330
to each of our speakers

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.870
in the order in which they will speak.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.850
And you should know that in the chat,

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.300
we also have links to their full bios

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.320
if you are interested

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.323
in having that information at this point.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.840
Our first speaker will be Rod Ferguson

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.760
who's a professor of women's
gender and sexuality studies

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.790
and American studies at Yale University.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.110
Ferguson's research and teaching interests

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.610
include the politics of culture,
women of color feminism,

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.950
the study of race, critical
university studies,

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.090
queer social movements, and social theory.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.640
Matthew Guterl is professor
of Africana Studies

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:13.680
and American Studies,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.370
and the Chair of American
Studies at Brown.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.750
He's a historian of race and nation

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.223
with a focus on the U.S., from
the Civil War to the present.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:27.280
Marci Kwon is an assistant
professor of Art and Art History

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.950
at Stanford University.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.760
Her research and teaching
interests include the intersection

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.340
of fine art and vernacular
practice, theories of modernism,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.250
cultural exchange between
Asia and the Americas,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.373
folk and self-taught art, and
issues of race and objecthood.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:48.200
Noémie Ndiyae is an assistant
professor of English

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.490
at the University of Chicago.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.130
Her research and teaching
explores the relationship

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.690
between theater understood
simultaneously as a medium,

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.420
a practice, an industry, an
institution, a social force,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.760
and a vibrant malleable
set of literary forms,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.160
and the social political
cultural struggles

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.453
of early modernity.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.180
And finally, we will
hear from Teresa Montoya,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.690
who is the Provost Postdoctoral Fellow,

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:17.890
and in 2022 will be an assistant
professor of anthropology

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.020
at the University of Chicago.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.460
She's a social scientist and media maker

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.160
trained in socio-cultural anthropology,

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.390
critical indigenous
studies, and filmmaking.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.450
Please join me in welcoming,
virtually, our panelists.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.627
And we will now hear from
Rod Ferguson. Thank you.

00:02:37.765 --> 00:02:39.660
- All right. Well, thanks so much.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:40.900
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.250
I'm gonna set my watch for five minutes

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.513
so that I don't go over.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.830
For me, the question
of the racial reckoning

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.470
with the disciplines

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:00.470
begins with the question of the
reorganization of knowledge,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:06.003
one, and its relationship
to the cultivation

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.100
of underrepresented and
new communities of subjects

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.463
within the university,

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.448
within, and also outside
of the discipline.

00:03:18.448 --> 00:03:21.160
And I'll say more about that a bit later.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.640
But this was something
that was driven home for me

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.440
when I was a grad student at UC San Diego

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.210
being advised by George
Lipsetts and Lisa Lowe.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.810
There was something about that moment

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.800
in which things clicked for me,

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.100
that the work that were doing

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.210
was not simply a textual exercise, right,

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:50.210
that it had institutional and
epistemological implications,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.290
things were supposed to
shift, that the work, in fact,

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.020
the work that we were doing

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.240
in our dissertations, for the
professors, and their books,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.370
it was to announce both epistemic ships

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.540
and also institutional shifts

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:13.210
within fields, on campuses,

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:17.390
in terms of faculty, and
also in terms of students.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:21.700
And so, that was a
profound revelation for me,

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:25.780
that it wasn't simply a kind
of professional exercise,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.753
the study of race and its
impact on the disciplines,

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.220
it wasn't simply a kind
of professional exercise

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.910
that was used to satisfy
certain benchmarks in terms of

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.400
the awarding of a PhD, or promotion,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.590
that it was about reorganizing

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.590
the taken-for-granted knowledges
within certain fields,

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.820
introducing other forms of
knowledge within those fields,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.820
and also making space for
underrepresented people,

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.063
new subjects, new communities,

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:11.570
who would also take up the
question or the act of reckoning,

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.880
and offer, really, new
kinds of innovations

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.280
at the level of scholarship.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.530
There is a passage

00:05:23.381 --> 00:05:28.381
in Jacques Derrida's the
"Eyes of the University"

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.540
that kind of summed it up for me.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.360
I'd always had that sense, again,

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:39.310
from my time at UC San Diego,
the work that I was doing

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.840
with my colleagues there,

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.350
the advising that I was getting

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:47.840
from people like George and Lisa,

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.010
but it wasn't until I encountered

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.490
this passage from Derrida,

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:58.490
that I had language for the institutional

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:03.347
and social importance of
the work that we were doing.

00:06:03.347 --> 00:06:06.460
And so, I'll just read a bit
from that passage, all right?

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:09.060
He says, "With students
in the research community

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.640
in every operation we pursue together,

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.800
a reading, an interpretation,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.090
the construction of a theoretical model,

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:18.300
the rhetoric of an argumentation,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.480
the treatment of historical material,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.450
and even a mathematical formalization,

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:27.550
we posit or acknowledge that
an institutional concept

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.040
is at play, a type of contract signed,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.100
an image of the ideal seminar constructed,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.090
a socius implied, repeated, or displaced,

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:41.160
invented, transformed,
threatened, or destroyed.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.080
An institution is not merely a few walls

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.270
or some outer structures
surrounding, protecting,

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:50.690
guaranteeing, or restricting
the freedom of our work,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.010
it is also, and already,

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.740
the structure of our interpretation."

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:00.120
And so, part of what I
find so useful about that

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.120
is that it gets at the
immediacy of the reckoning,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.620
that the reckoning happens at the moment

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:12.520
that we're thinking about
the book we're gonna write,

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.803
the course we're gonna teach,
how we frame those things,

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:22.500
and that in those very
immediate and small acts,

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:27.510
we're actually trying to
either repeat a discipline

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.470
or displace

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:34.870
disciplinary knowledge

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:37.530
in the name of alternative
forms of knowledge.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.630
All right, so, I'll stop there. Thank you.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:42.620
- I have spent much of the pandemic

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.860
thinking about a 40-year-old novel,

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.360
Octavia Butler's "Kindred",

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.750
and I wanna talk a little bit about it

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.240
before I share my thoughts
about the humanities.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.410
Published in 1979, the
book tells the story

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:58.490
of a Black woman named Dana
who suddenly, inexplicably,

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.010
and repeatedly transported
back to the Antebellum South

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.930
to save her ancestor, a White
slave owner named Rufus.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.770
Whenever Rufus' life is threatened,

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:11.970
Dana is yanked backwards in
time without warning or control.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:14.430
Her reappearances,
though, keep Rufus alive

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.820
and, by extension, ensure
her very existence.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.260
And by the novel's conclusion,

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.040
Dana is fundamentally changed
by this recursive experience,

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.890
leading us to wonder about
causality and inevitability.

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:29.160
The world around her notably remains

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.563
just as disturbingly uneven
as it was at the start.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.750
But Dana is a hero for our pandemic.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.070
Nonplussed by her time traveling,

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:40.940
she's practical and level-headed,

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.230
routinely confronts terror
with thoughtful planning.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:45.860
After her first dislocation,

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.840
she returns home and has the forethought

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.030
to wisely assemble a bag
of necessary supplies,

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.920
tying them to her waist.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.700
She brings maps and history books

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:57.690
from the present into the past,

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.600
along with knives and painkillers,

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.170
soap, toothpaste, and clothing.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:06.450
Like the author who created
her, Dana is defined by lists

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.490
and is a champion of organized thinking

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:09.923
in the face of calamity.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:14.440
This faith in relentless
practicality saves her life.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.340
The knife she brings back in time,

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.330
the kind of weapon denied
to any enslaved person,

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:21.780
the kind of weapon she
knew she might need,

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:25.323
is what she uses to kill Rufus
in the novel's final pages.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:29.520
I've shared "Kindred" more
times than I can count

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.730
in the last 12 months.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:32.563
My deepest hope has been

00:09:32.563 --> 00:09:35.220
that the students who are
now zooming into our meetups

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.990
from bedrooms, kitchen tables, and patios,

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.010
in almost every conceivable time zone

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:44.000
and who might feel displaced,
buffeted, and even scared,

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.010
can find, in Dana's steely determination,

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:50.223
a practical model for their
own everyday survival.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.010
There's a valuable
lesson here for us, too,

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.510
as we ponder the relationship

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.413
between race and the humanities.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:00.790
All of Dana's courage and imagination

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:03.800
inevitably leads her
right back to the present.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.800
There is no other timeline
and no counterfactual reality.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.670
Her movements back and forth
from the Antebellum South

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:14.210
to the modern present
change nothing in her world.

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:17.500
She gains memories, a measure of herself,

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:21.030
and a sober understanding
of the realities of slavery.

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:23.390
But she loses something, too.

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.110
After Rufus' death, she materializes back

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:28.060
in present day California,

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.240
her arm painfully adjoined
to the timbers and nails

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.630
and plaster of the house.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:36.973
Its removal sets her free,
but leaves her without a limb.

00:10:37.937 --> 00:10:40.380
"I really couldn't let her
come all the way back,"

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.117
Butler once explained to Randall Kenan.

00:10:43.117 --> 00:10:45.560
"I couldn't let her
return to what she was.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:47.427
I couldn't let her come back whole."

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:50.360
When I was first asked to consider

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.350
the idea of a racial reckoning,

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:55.210
I returned once more to "Kindred".

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:59.310
A reckoning, I know, is a
dramatic settlement of debts.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:03.960
It can be a jarring event, a
disruptive, painful occurrence.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.183
It should cost.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.490
And who should bear that cost?

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.620
I thought of field-locked
history departments

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.560
and English departments
unable to truly center

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.470
racial justice in their work

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.370
because of an old-fashioned commitment

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.900
to geographic or temporal divisions

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.630
that date back to Jim Crow,

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:24.220
to White supremacy, or to empire.

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:27.100
I thought of entire disciplines,
classics, for instance,

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:29.850
or medieval studies,
where the labor of decades

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:33.190
is now the foundation
stone of White supremacy.

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.070
I thought of the small inter-disciplines,

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.450
many of them zeroed in on race,

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:39.770
all of them starved for resources

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:42.800
compared to their larger
peers, and overdetermined

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:47.380
as the place where race
happens, or is, or will be.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:49.650
I thought of budget models like RCM,

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:52.140
which valorize student credit hours

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:55.140
over righteousness or social importance.

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.330
I thought of all of this and so much more

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:00.907
and asked, "Exactly what sort
of reckoning should there be?"

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:05.950
Such questions come to me,
even as I am overwhelmed,

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:09.020
joyfully and ecstatically,
by the volume of work

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:11.370
in the humanities on race.

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:16.120
I am drowning in books, in
podcasts, in graphic novels,

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:20.370
in think pieces, in documentaries,
and in Twitter threads,

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:24.210
surrounded, at home and at
work, by disorganized piles,

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:29.030
alphabetical piles, thematic
piles, and color-coded piles.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:32.650
Every single one of them,
evidence that the dam has burst,

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:35.030
that we are, in some very perverse way,

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:38.370
fortunate to be writing and
thinking through all of this,

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:40.850
our own dystopia together,

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:44.560
with such a vast and extraordinary
assemblage of people,

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:48.340
authors of some of the
best work ever conceived.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:51.030
What would it mean to
center this everywhere?

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:52.340
How could that happen?

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:56.130
And what would that cost the humanities?

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:57.337
To paraphrase Butler,

00:12:57.337 --> 00:13:00.640
"After a true reckoning
with our past and present,

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:03.967
would we want to bring the
university all the way back?"

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.830
The humanities will
someday have a reckoning,

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:11.410
and that reckoning will, I
think, tear these places apart

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:14.700
and leave them conceptually
burning and broken.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.990
There's some promise in
our perspective losses,

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:21.950
in what parts of these
places might be cut off.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:25.180
And we in the Zoom should
all be planning for it

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:27.540
with knapsacks tied to our waists

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.060
filled with old maps and history books,

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:34.174
painkillers and knives,
and soaps, and toothpastes.

00:13:34.174 --> 00:13:37.970
Thank you.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:39.427
- Hi, everyone.

00:13:39.427 --> 00:13:41.793
Thank you for having me here today.

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:45.853
Can't do anything without images, so.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.640
So, this year, the Cantor Arts Center,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.133
which is the University Art
Museum here at Stanford,

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.190
launched the Asian-American
Art Initiative.

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.930
Co-directed by myself and Elisa Alexander,

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:02.900
Curator of American Art,

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:07.560
the Initiative is dedicated to
the collection, preservation,

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:10.450
research, and exhibition
of work by Asian-American

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:12.400
and diaspora artists,

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:16.190
who, with the exception of a
handful of canonical figures,

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:19.683
remain almost entirely
unknown in art history.

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:22.900
The dearth of interest and support

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.190
for Asian-American
artists within art history

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:29.730
stands in direct contrast
to the historical importance

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.923
of the arts to Asian-Americans.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:35.460
While researching the 2009 survey tax

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:39.570
to "Asian American Art:
A History, 1850-1970",

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.280
Mark Dean Johnson, who
actually worked on this project

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:47.280
as a fellow at CSRE, and
Stanford historian, Gordon Chang,

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:52.240
identified over 1,000
artists of Asian descent

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:56.910
working in California alone
during this chronological stand.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:59.433
which is, again, an 1850 to 1970.

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:03.020
So, this number is staggering,

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:05.650
indicating not only
the significance of art

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:08.110
and art-making for Asian-Americans,

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:10.210
but also the enormous volume of work

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:13.700
that remains to be studied and
has likely been lost to time.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:15.908
And what you see on
your screen is actually,

00:15:15.908 --> 00:15:18.760
they're photographs
from May's Photo Studio,

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:22.050
which was a Chinese-run photography studio

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:27.050
in San Francisco Chinatown,
akin to James Van Der Zee,

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:29.150
photographing the community.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:31.320
And these works were saved, actually,

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:36.290
from a dumpster in the 1970s,
by the artist Wiley Wong.

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:38.860
What you see here are ma-rk-ets,

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:40.880
photographs that were
actually pieced together

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:45.363
in the dark room, kind of uniting
families that were divided

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:52.210
by American legal, like,
exclusionary immigration policy.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.140
So, we launched this
year with a collection

00:15:57.140 --> 00:16:00.360
that includes 151 historical works

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.280
from the estate of a private collector,

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:05.503
which were being held in a
storage locker in San Rafael,

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:09.590
which you see on the screen on the left.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:12.810
We also received donations
from foundations of artists,

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:14.950
such as Martin Wong and Bernie Spain,

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:17.300
whose work you see on the right.

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:18.830
It's actually covered in plastic,

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:20.350
which is why it looks like that.

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:23.660
On the left is a painting
from the Brown collection

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:28.260
by the first documented
Japanese-American painter

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:29.513
in the United States.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:35.760
And we also acquired an
untitled wall of masks

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:37.850
by the artist, Ruth Asawa,

00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:42.690
who cast them from the faces
of visitors to her home.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:45.423
And they include people
like Anna Deavere Smith.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:51.160
We have also made it a priority
to ensure that we preserve

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.193
archival material from these artists.

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:57.740
So, Asian-American studies
has helped me think through

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:01.070
the ethical dimensions of this project.

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:04.900
It is not lost on me that part
of our history's conservatism

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:08.340
has to do with its emphasis
on physical objects,

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:10.410
objects that are bought and sold,

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.520
making it more obviously
entangled with market forces

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.573
than other academic disciplines.

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:18.160
This has also made art history

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.060
especially reliant on
institutions, such as museums,

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:25.060
to undertake the extensive
and expensive conservation,

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.823
preservation, and
display, of works of art.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:30.720
And finally, there's the problem

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.640
with the way that race
and identity discourse

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.213
are mobilized by institutions
as a means to manage descent,

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:38.990
an issue that Rod's work,

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:43.770
as well as Riley's edited
volume with hin-tai-gap

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:45.223
has helped me think through.

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:48.090
So, we've tried to navigate

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.853
these ethical challenges in multiple ways.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.210
In our discussions of the
Asian-American Art Initiative,

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:58.140
we always begin with the
fact that Stanford is itself

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:00.360
unthinkable without the entwined histories

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:03.110
of Chinese immigrant labor
imperialist expansion

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:06.750
and capitalism, as
ma-noo-koo-roo-kar has shown.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:09.280
And in fact, The Golden Last Spike

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:12.760
and the silver mallet used
to hammer it into place,

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:14.910
the joining of the
transcontinental railroad,

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.693
are at the Cantor Arts Center.

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:21.070
Recent archeological
work has also revealed

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:23.730
that even the palm trees on Palm Drive

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:26.103
were planted by Chinese laborers.

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.110
So, another goal of our work

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:31.070
is to show how historical research

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.130
and deep formal engagement
with works of art

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:36.390
breaks rather than reinforces

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:38.740
a monolithic idea of Asian-America

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.030
as a bounded identity forum,

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:47.030
as well as its assumptions
of a single-nation state.

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:51.240
And rather than keeping our
work in the feedback loop

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:54.800
with academia, the
museum, and the art world,

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:57.510
we're trying to make sure that
we engage local communities

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:00.233
in part, through education
and public programming,

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:04.920
and most importantly,
we know that our efforts

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:08.550
cannot benefit just the
community of Asian-Americans,

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.160
but all racialized groups
whose histories and lives

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:13.140
are entangled with ours.

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:16.470
And I wanna acknowledge
that CSRE and Jen Brody,

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:19.050
in particular, have been
unfailingly supportive

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:20.333
of these efforts.

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:25.850
So, still I must admit that
I have serious reservations

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:28.070
about how our work will be used,

00:19:28.070 --> 00:19:29.870
something that keeps me up at night.

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:34.110
Museums are, of course,
colonial institutions

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:36.940
built on the hoarding of
property and increasingly reliant

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:39.073
on the commodification of people of color.

00:19:40.630 --> 00:19:43.620
All I can say is that I believe
the work of these artists

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:46.300
have many things to teach
us about the structures

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:49.760
that have attempted to
circumscribe their existence.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:53.620
I can only imagine the things
they will teach us about,

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:56.000
for example, racial
capitalism and aesthetics,

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:58.630
the way, quote, art has
been historically tethered

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:00.560
to the idea of the human

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:03.283
and hierarchies of tastes and value.

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:08.023
Our history should be the
study of all of these things.

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:11.220
But also, as an Anne Cheng has pointed out

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.633
more than 20 years ago,
and continues to remind us,

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:17.800
in the United States, a person of color

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.330
is seen as worthy of attention

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:22.430
only when they have suffered visible harm.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:25.230
While Asian-American artists

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:27.300
have never been outside of history,

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.210
they invite us to think and feel

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:34.210
in ways that perhaps sidestep
this obsession with injury.

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:39.020
Their work shows us the
multiple and complex ways

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:41.620
Asian-Americans have lived, worked,

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:44.120
created, desired, and imagined,

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:47.150
in relation to a world that
seeks evermore devious ways

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:49.230
to homogenize and devalue them.

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:50.063
Thank you.

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:57.870
- Thank you. Hello, everybody.

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:00.940
I am thrilled to be here in the
presence of such brilliance.

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:04.410
And I'm really excited to
learn from everybody today.

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:07.680
I hope to contribute something
to this ongoing conversation

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.050
by telling you a little bit

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:11.550
about the current state of my field,

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:13.660
early modern critical race studies,

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:15.320
and telling you about its current states

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:19.030
through the lens of our key
concept, racial reckonings.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:20.570
So, what do we do?

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:22.440
Early modern critical race scholars

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:25.200
examine the deployment
of racial formations

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.150
in periods prior to the development

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:29.470
of so-called scientific racism

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:33.310
in the 18th century, starting in 1492.

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:35.460
And we do so in various cultural domains,

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:38.340
such as literature, performance, religion,

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:41.710
visual culture, material
culture, and more.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:44.610
The word reckoning, as
was pointed out earlier,

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:48.320
is etymologically embedded
in practices of accounting,

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:49.550
it's about numbers.

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:52.830
It's about counting, putting
an order, calculating,

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:54.110
and including in that count,

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:57.440
what must be included and accounted for.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:58.670
The pioneers of my field

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:01.020
had to counter the convenient assumption

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:04.120
that there were no BIPOC
people in early modern Europe,

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:05.740
or differently put,

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:07.930
that Shakespeare had
never seen a Black person

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:10.750
when he wrote "Othello", quote unquote.

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:11.710
In order to make a case

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:13.750
where the importance of racial thinking

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:17.950
in early modern Europe, we
are indebted to the subfield

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:20.010
of presence studies best illustrated

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:23.280
by the painstaking work
of the late Imtiaz Habib

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:26.470
and his marvelous "Black
Lives in the English Archives"

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:28.403
for some serious reckoning.

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:32.943
For the field can only grow, I'm sorry,

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:34.890
for the field to grow,

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.340
reckoning in the
accountant's sense applied,

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:38.173
and still applies,

00:22:38.173 --> 00:22:41.000
not only to the archives,
but to those who read them.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:42.860
It has thus been an
imperative in our field

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:45.500
to reckon with the dismal
numbers of BIPOC scholars

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:47.060
in early modern studies.

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:49.940
The most English curricula, as you know,

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:51.560
have a Shakespeare requirement.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:55.030
But to the best of my knowledge,
there are only, currently,

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:58.770
less than 20 tenure-track faculty members

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:01.510
who identify as Black
Shakespeare professors

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:03.630
in the U.S. at the moment.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.650
We also have had, and still have,

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:07.780
to start building diverse pipelines,

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:10.590
which is an ongoing struggle
because BIPOC students

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:12.650
are conditioned, or maybe disciplined,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:14.210
from high school onwards

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:16.490
into thinking of the pre-modern periods

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:18.830
as irrelevant to their concerns.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:21.830
Of course, that is a misconception.

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:23.110
We know that Stuart Hall

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:25.440
originally wanted to be a medievalist

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:27.360
and that Sylvia Wynter, for instance,

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:29.730
was primarily trained as a specialist

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.453
of early modern Spanish drama.

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:35.610
In short, racial reckonings in my field

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:38.570
have been about changing both
the objects that we study

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:41.930
and the demographics of
scholars who study them.

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:44.660
But, of course, a disciplinary
field can only be transformed

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.450
if its theoretical frameworks,
tools, and questions,

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:49.300
are transformed as well.

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:52.610
Early modern critical race
studies emerged historically

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:56.180
in the mid '90s when
scholars like Kim F. Hall

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:59.520
brought theoretical frameworks
drawn from Black feminism

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:03.610
and critical race theory to
bear, on early modern materials,

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:06.700
and to make early modern
studies accountable.

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:08.820
Ania Loomba, around the
same time, did the same

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:12.680
with frameworks drawn from
post-colonial feminism.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:14.270
The second wave of scholarship on race

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:15.450
in the early modern period

00:24:15.450 --> 00:24:18.190
started in the first
decade of the 21st century,

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:21.000
and we are now in the third, or fourth,

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:22.570
depending on how you count,

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:24.620
wave of scholarship in the field.

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:26.470
Yet valid concerns have been expressed

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:30.017
by field founders, Kim F. Hall,
and Peter Erickson, in 2016,

00:24:30.017 --> 00:24:32.060
in the 2016 special issue

00:24:32.060 --> 00:24:34.800
of "Shakespeare Quarterly",
focused on race.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:36.640
And they alerted us, in that issue,

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:39.130
to the risks of dispersion,

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:40.910
to the very real risks that the field,

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:43.860
even as it starts receiving
institutional recognition

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:47.420
in various forms, might
lose its political impetus

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:51.120
and its original concern
with racial justice.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:53.630
To honor and to act
upon the activist drive

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:55.030
that founded the field,

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:57.280
a research collaborative,
such as RaceB4Race,

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:00.450
which was founded by Ayana Thompson,

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:05.180
and U Chicago is a partner
institution of RaceB4Race,

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:08.340
that collaborative has, for
the last couple of years,

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:11.070
advocated, in various
forums, for the centrality

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:13.570
of critical race theory to the field.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:14.930
If you're curious to hear more,

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:18.200
I encourage you to check
the webpage of RaceB4Race.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:21.600
Our next symposium, I'm sorry
I have to make a quick plug,

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:24.320
our next symposium focused on politics,

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:27.120
that's the theme, with
a keynote by Ibram Kendi

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:29.200
opens this coming Tuesday.

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:31.020
But to return to my point,

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:34.250
racial reckoning is about
political accountability,

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:36.430
which must be intentionally cultivated

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:38.363
at all times, in my field.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:41.260
We were asked to think about the ways

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:44.390
in which we center racial
justice in our work,

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:46.980
and it is perhaps for research,

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:48.143
the research part of our work,

00:25:48.143 --> 00:25:50.890
that the question is most urgent.

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:53.130
An example might be useful at this point.

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:54.930
In my most recent article,

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:58.210
making my work politically
accountable to the present

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:01.140
has meant that when I
started noticing slippages

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:02.740
in 17th-century plays

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:05.190
which depict and label the same characters

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:09.700
alternately as Blackamoors
and Romani gypsies,

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:14.050
I had to think about those
early modern slippages

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:17.290
through the lens of the
many instances today

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:19.470
when scholars working
in the fledgling field

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:21.310
of critical Romani studies,

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:23.290
invoke paradigms from Black studies

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:26.590
in pursuit of shared liberation.

00:26:26.590 --> 00:26:28.510
This led me to read the
early modern archive

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:31.470
as providing an ethically
binding genealogy

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:35.603
for those critical alliances,
which are reconstituting now.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:39.240
Methodologically speaking,
doing such work requires

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:42.500
never forgetting about the
present as you analyze the past,

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:45.090
developing some sort of double vision,

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:50.090
moving to and fro between
historization and theorization.

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:53.830
Racial reckonings also
transform disciplinary affects.

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:55.810
To do this work, you have to keep in mind

00:26:55.810 --> 00:26:57.440
that the stakes of the work,

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:00.620
as Rod was pointing out earlier,

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:02.560
the stakes of the work, which are surreal,

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:04.650
they outweigh the playful dimension

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:06.170
of aesthetic exploration,

00:27:06.170 --> 00:27:09.020
which is one of the main
appeals of literary studies.

00:27:09.020 --> 00:27:10.480
And you have to pay close attention

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:13.850
to the ways in which paranoid
or reparative leanings

00:27:13.850 --> 00:27:17.400
might inform your own
scholarship and its reception,

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:19.520
because those matter.

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:21.350
I will close by saying that

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:24.260
working with theoretical
bodies of knowledge

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:27.620
produced in other disciplines,
such as critical race theory,

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:29.420
Black studies, sociology,

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:32.030
and in my case, performance
studies and dance studies,

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:36.280
is a move that is welcome
in English studies.

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:39.610
English has historically been
characterized as a discipline

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:43.020
by anti-disciplinary drives,
at least in the departments

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:44.965
where I have had the good fortune to work,

00:27:44.965 --> 00:27:47.550
and in very recent years.

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:49.370
Thus, I have personally found English

00:27:49.370 --> 00:27:54.060
to be a good non-disciplining
discipline to operate from.

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.040
But I do think that
the future developments

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:58.080
of early modern critical race studies

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:00.860
require expanding in transnational

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:02.469
and multi-linguistic directions,

00:28:02.469 --> 00:28:05.840
so, taking the project in a
com-plar direction, if you will,

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:08.800
because the invention of
Whiteness in the pre-modern era

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:10.780
was a transnational endeavor.

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:14.050
So, such extension is in
tune with the transnational

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:15.510
and diasporic dynamics

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:18.090
that have always been at
the core of Black studies.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.610
And I believe that the study
of race along those lines

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:22.480
can help early modern English studies

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:26.680
move past the Anglocentrism
that has, for so long,

00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:29.640
limited their purview and potential.

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:30.473
Thank you.

00:28:33.096 --> 00:28:35.410
- (speaks in foreign language)
Thank you to the organizers

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:37.807
for convening this important dialogue.

00:28:37.807 --> 00:28:40.820
For my indigenous colleagues
who are tuning in,

00:28:40.820 --> 00:28:44.250
I will also introduce
myself as a Dene woman.

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:45.659
(speaks in foreign
language) Teresa Montoya

00:28:45.659 --> 00:28:49.409
(speaks in foreign language)

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:56.050
My so-called interracial heritage

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:59.990
is revealed in the introduction
of my four Dene clans.

00:28:59.990 --> 00:29:03.380
My mother's clan is followed
by my non-Native father

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:06.033
of Mexican and German-American ancestry.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.350
But unlike Euro-American categories

00:29:09.350 --> 00:29:12.180
of racial and ethnic classification,

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:14.120
Dene and indigenous epistemologies

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:16.440
are shared in order to find connection,

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:18.240
identify a means of relation,

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:20.660
and also responsibility to each other,

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:22.343
rather than social difference.

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:25.050
It is perhaps of this knowledge

00:29:25.050 --> 00:29:27.310
so ingrained in my identity formation,

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:29.870
living in, and between
my mother's birth home

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:33.210
on Navajo Nation, and the
offers of Asian places

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:34.290
that she carried me,

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:38.183
that curiosities of my own
racialized difference took hold.

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:42.710
For many BIPOC scholars, our
histories and vulnerabilities

00:29:42.710 --> 00:29:44.580
are what drives the work that we do

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:48.420
and also what makes it so
difficult because our bodies,

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:51.250
our skin, and our
histories, can also become

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:54.083
uncomfortable points of
critical interrogation.

00:29:55.720 --> 00:29:57.820
Increasingly, I'm finding strength

00:29:57.820 --> 00:29:59.850
in this radical vulnerability

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:01.430
as a way of reckoning my own place

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:04.610
in the trajectory of historically
White academic spaces

00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:08.120
the role that our disciplinary
founders, in quotes here,

00:30:08.120 --> 00:30:10.270
have played in shaping these discourses

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:13.490
and how this knowledge can
be employed in our pedagogy,

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:15.630
activism, and public engagements,

00:30:15.630 --> 00:30:19.040
to confront structures of
racism, White supremacy,

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:20.873
and settler-colonial violence.

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:24.330
So, I present this intimate portrait

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:28.210
as an invitation for us to
explore the differential ways

00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:30.710
that race is invoked in
discussions of Blackness

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:33.440
in indigeneity, in Asian studies,

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:35.270
and what connections might be forged

00:30:35.270 --> 00:30:38.540
between our overlapping
and unique struggles.

00:30:38.540 --> 00:30:41.103
These concerns, of course,
are by no means new.

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:43.730
In the discipline of anthropology,

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:45.310
in which I was trained, for instance,

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.670
scholars like Lee Baker have interrogated

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:50.290
how categories of culture were deployed

00:30:50.290 --> 00:30:53.610
to study the so-called
disappearing Indians

00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:56.550
by early American ethnologists

00:30:56.550 --> 00:31:00.140
while categories of race for
use by early sociologists

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:02.820
to study African-Americans.

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:05.710
The problems of classification
and differentiation

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:07.680
are not only a disciplinary one,

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:10.933
but also a trademark of
colonial and imperial power.

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:14.753
Consider now the category of Indian,

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:16.970
the fallacy of the title

00:31:16.970 --> 00:31:18.930
references a gross navigational error

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:21.830
by the mass murder of
Christopher Columbus.

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:23.480
As documented in his own journals,

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:25.960
the transatlantic voyage
of Columbus set in motion

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:28.903
centuries of European
colonization in the Americas.

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:30.970
Under his leadership,

00:31:30.970 --> 00:31:33.230
the indigenous population of Hispaniola,

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:35.410
now Haiti and the Dominican Republic,

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:40.410
estimated between 500,000
to 1 million people in 1492,

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:43.993
plummeted to 60,000 within
a single generation.

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:47.210
Following this violent encounter,

00:31:47.210 --> 00:31:50.460
European philosophers and
theologians began to consider

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:53.503
the morality and humanity
of the Native inhabitants

00:31:53.503 --> 00:31:55.363
that they, quote, discovered.

00:31:56.390 --> 00:32:00.070
Did they have souls?
Did they possess reason?

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:01.283
Could they be governed?

00:32:02.670 --> 00:32:05.300
Contrary to the assumption
that conquest was justified

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:07.600
through the construction
of radical alterity,

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:10.000
colonization under the Spanish empire,

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:11.810
legitimized through the Christian logic

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:14.230
of divine hierarchal power,

00:32:14.230 --> 00:32:17.250
made the Indian into
irrational and legible subject

00:32:17.250 --> 00:32:18.853
in order to be governable.

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:22.570
On the other hand, as argued by scholars,

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:23.650
such as Stuart Banner,

00:32:23.650 --> 00:32:26.250
the British were less
concerned about soul matter

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:30.550
or capacity for rationality
within a humanistic tradition.

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:32.880
Rather, the colonization of North America

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:35.700
required an initial recognition

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:38.240
of indigenous property ownership.

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:41.050
To the extent that the
English wanted Indian land,

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:42.850
than they would have to purchase it.

00:32:44.770 --> 00:32:46.930
The recognition of Native
property, it seems,

00:32:46.930 --> 00:32:50.143
was only in the moment
in the sale transaction.

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:54.300
This larger notion of
political recognition

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:56.900
engaged by scholars,
such as Audra Simpson,

00:32:56.900 --> 00:32:58.847
Glen Coulthard, Beth ko-ven-alee

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:02.900
and property, is central to
ongoing political debates

00:33:02.900 --> 00:33:05.730
over indigenous sovereignty
and their right to existence

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:08.100
in settler-colonial states of Australia,

00:33:08.100 --> 00:33:10.993
New Zealand, Canada, and
the United States today.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:14.590
Therefore, to reckon with the violence

00:33:14.590 --> 00:33:15.990
of racial classification

00:33:15.990 --> 00:33:19.370
is also to confront the persistent
structures of Whiteness,

00:33:19.370 --> 00:33:22.130
which, as Aboriginal scholar,
Eileen Warren Robertson,

00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:26.050
argues, is rooted within
logics of possession,

00:33:26.050 --> 00:33:28.430
whether it be owning property,

00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:31.883
being property, or becoming property-less.

00:33:32.930 --> 00:33:35.130
These ideas around property and possession

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:39.160
form the basis of a settler
and a White national identity,

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:41.740
an identity that many
Native scholars have argued

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:45.460
is also predicated on the
dispossession of indigenous land,

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:48.210
the dilution of indigenous
blood and identity

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:50.260
through blood quantum politics,

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:53.420
as critically explored by
Lakota scholar, Kim TallBear,

00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:55.593
and Osage scholar, Jean Dennison,

00:33:56.630 --> 00:33:59.540
and the erasure of limitations
of indigenous sovereignty

00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:01.113
in federal Indian policy.

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:05.557
So, to bring us back to the
title of our panel today,

00:34:05.557 --> 00:34:09.260
"Racial Reckonings", I'd like
for all of us to consider

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:11.100
the tangible ways we can build upon

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:12.860
existing race conversations

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:16.160
to imagine how we might
translate these disciplinary

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:19.200
and historical differences
into greater solidarity

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:20.940
around movements for reparations

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:23.823
and land acknowledgements
in our respective campuses.

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:27.720
This is, by no means, an
end goal, but rather one way

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:30.520
to demand a radically different
relationship of universities

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:33.064
with their histories and those they serve.

00:34:33.064 --> 00:34:34.723
Thank you, and (speaks
in foreign language).

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:41.210
- Thank you, everyone.

00:34:41.210 --> 00:34:44.640
Those were phenomenal opening comments

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:47.930
with so much depth and complexity.

00:34:47.930 --> 00:34:50.820
Usually, I have lots
of synthetic questions.

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:53.840
I have a couple (laughs)
because I'm still digesting.

00:34:53.840 --> 00:34:55.620
That was amazing.

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:57.310
I wanna open, if you don't mind,

00:34:57.310 --> 00:35:01.410
with a very broad question
that I think most of you,

00:35:01.410 --> 00:35:06.210
if not all of you, sort
of touched indirectly on

00:35:07.252 --> 00:35:10.440
in your comments, at one point or another.

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:12.310
And so, let me ask the question

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:14.070
and then I'll see if I
can remind the audience

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:17.233
of a few of the examples
of when it came up.

00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:20.370
So, my sort of broad

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:23.803
sort of intentionally a little
bit provocative question is,

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:28.400
do you think the disciplinary structures

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:30.650
that we are grappling with

00:35:30.650 --> 00:35:35.330
are capable of being

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:37.580
a part of the transformations,

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:39.900
being a productive part
of the transformations

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:44.020
that your collective work
is speaking to, right?

00:35:44.020 --> 00:35:48.330
That is to say, are they
going to be blown up?

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:49.990
Matt sort of has an image

00:35:49.990 --> 00:35:51.600
that they're going to disintegrate,

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:53.543
I'm looking forward to seeing that.

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:57.350
Rod talked a bit about, right,

00:35:57.350 --> 00:36:00.010
the ways in which there's an institutional

00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:02.983
and epistemological challenge,
which implies, right,

00:36:02.983 --> 00:36:06.053
that things are going to have
to be dramatically changed.

00:36:07.712 --> 00:36:11.590
Marci's talking about the
role of this kind of the art,

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:15.440
and bringing these objects
into places that are colonial,

00:36:15.440 --> 00:36:18.050
but also transforming that relationship,

00:36:18.050 --> 00:36:19.810
as three quick examples.

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:24.810
But are these institutions
really capable of this?

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:27.490
I guess I wanna push a few of you,

00:36:27.490 --> 00:36:30.340
if you're interested in taking
the challenge, to sort of

00:36:32.650 --> 00:36:34.090
really explore that, right?

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:35.750
I mean, I understand what we're doing

00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:37.550
in relation to these institutions,

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:40.680
but when we're in our relative cubbies,

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:43.300
it doesn't seem like a lot
of disruptions happening.

00:36:43.300 --> 00:36:45.530
When we're together in a cohort,

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:49.750
there seems like a lot of
disruption of amazing scholars.

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:52.160
But there's a lot of
people doing this work

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:55.630
in the way that we would be challenged by.

00:36:55.630 --> 00:36:58.270
So, I'm just throwing it open.

00:36:58.270 --> 00:37:00.360
I don't know, anyone, I'm open to anyone.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:02.680
I think I mentioned Marci, Matt, and Rod,

00:37:02.680 --> 00:37:04.520
so, maybe one of you could start us off,

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:06.813
and then everyone is open to engage.

00:37:12.009 --> 00:37:13.170
- [Rod] Okay, well maybe I'll go.

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:14.837
- Okay. Thanks, Rod.

00:37:14.837 --> 00:37:17.920
- (laughs) Pleasure.

00:37:19.750 --> 00:37:24.590
I think it's difficult for every field

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:29.900
within a university because,
especially the disciplines,

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:32.000
I mean, even the category, the discipline,

00:37:32.840 --> 00:37:34.840
is in and of itself,

00:37:34.840 --> 00:37:39.680
it means a resistance
to change, innovation,

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:41.010
shifts, blah, blah, blah.

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:43.300
And it's not as if the inner disciplines

00:37:43.300 --> 00:37:44.760
are immune from that

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:49.020
because they're also
subject to those logics.

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:50.593
At the same time,

00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:56.420
even with the problems of
the interdisciplinary fields,

00:37:56.420 --> 00:37:59.300
I would put my money on
the inter-disciplines

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:04.300
over the disciplines in
terms of the kinds of changes

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:06.420
that you're talking about, right?

00:38:06.420 --> 00:38:07.760
If you look at

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:13.660
where are the minoritized
faculty hired, located, students,

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:19.823
underrepresented students
in terms of majors, located,

00:38:21.670 --> 00:38:22.830
it's in the inter-disciplines.

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:24.565
I mean, I'll never forget a conference,

00:38:24.565 --> 00:38:28.060
or a symposium that I went to years ago,

00:38:28.060 --> 00:38:31.550
I think like in 2011, at UCLA,

00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:34.730
between the UCLA Center
for the Study of Women

00:38:34.730 --> 00:38:37.080
and NYU's counterpart, they
had a joint conference.

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:41.500
And Kathleen McHugh, who was the Director

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:44.920
of the Center at UCLA, had a slide up

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:51.410
showing where all of the
underrepresented faculty were

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:53.760
in the University,

00:38:53.760 --> 00:38:56.820
and they were all in
the inter-disciplines.

00:38:56.820 --> 00:38:59.700
So, the question was, if we
took away the inter-disciplines,

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:01.870
what would then happen
to those faculty members?

00:39:01.870 --> 00:39:03.793
They would be gone, okay?

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:07.643
That's one example.

00:39:07.643 --> 00:39:09.823
If you think about another example,

00:39:11.830 --> 00:39:13.800
I'm a bit biased here, but
I also think it's true,

00:39:13.800 --> 00:39:16.380
the American Studies Association, right,

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:19.450
and all of the sort of
epistemic revolutions

00:39:19.450 --> 00:39:22.230
that field has gone through,

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:25.950
from the immediate end of World War II

00:39:26.990 --> 00:39:31.300
in terms of American studies
really being an alibi

00:39:31.300 --> 00:39:34.130
for the U.S. during the Cold War period,

00:39:34.130 --> 00:39:36.380
but also then being changed

00:39:36.380 --> 00:39:40.180
by the social movements
around race and gender,

00:39:40.180 --> 00:39:42.480
and then being changed again

00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:47.480
by the critique of U.S.
empire and globalization,

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:52.600
thinking here of that "Cultures
of U.S. Imperialism" book

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:55.230
from Amy Kaplan and
Don P., that anthology,

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:56.560
but then changed again

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:58.601
in terms of post-nationalist
American studies,

00:39:58.601 --> 00:40:03.601
then changing again because
of the work of scholars

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:08.610
in disability studies, in queer
studies, in trans studies,

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:10.645
and in Indigenous studies.

00:40:10.645 --> 00:40:15.583
So, we can actually point to instances

00:40:17.810 --> 00:40:22.467
in which the inter-disciplines have been

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:28.760
more ready to enact the kind of changes

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:29.880
that you're talking about.

00:40:29.880 --> 00:40:31.920
There are still problems,

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:33.860
I don't wanna lose sight of that,

00:40:33.860 --> 00:40:38.380
but in terms of the
kinds of transformations,

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:40.283
both epistemic and social,

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:42.830
the inter-disciplines will carry the day,

00:40:42.830 --> 00:40:44.360
I mean, they carry that history.

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:46.860
- Mm-hmm, mm-hmm. Okay, all right.

00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:49.049
So, long live the inter-disciplines.

00:40:49.049 --> 00:40:50.980
- Long live the inter-disciplines.

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:53.493
- Matt, did you wanna
jump in, or Marci, or?

00:40:55.410 --> 00:40:57.683
- I'll buy Marci some time.

00:40:59.038 --> 00:41:03.193
I think, Tricia, to answer
your question directly,

00:41:04.780 --> 00:41:06.070
I think it's a question

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:07.717
of whether the disciplines should survive,

00:41:07.717 --> 00:41:10.430
whether the humanities
should survive as they are

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:13.960
in order to center racial justice,

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:15.970
or just race in the humanities.

00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:19.020
And I don't think they should,
I don't think they can.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:21.510
I think this is the second paragraph

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:22.760
after Rod's paragraph,

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:25.350
about the value of the inter-disciplines,

00:41:25.350 --> 00:41:26.770
is that the disciplines themselves

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:29.300
are inherently conservative and hegemonic,

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:34.300
and that we stand to have to
fight department by department,

00:41:35.030 --> 00:41:37.700
field by field, sub field by sub field,

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:40.210
in order to advance interests,

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:44.769
to get one hire, one faculty
member, one course taught,

00:41:44.769 --> 00:41:46.893
and that struggle will outlive us.

00:41:48.200 --> 00:41:49.930
We can't win that struggle.

00:41:49.930 --> 00:41:53.220
So, it is true that in some institutions,

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:55.390
so much of this is
university by university,

00:41:55.390 --> 00:41:57.180
like, I'm thinking about
my own history department

00:41:57.180 --> 00:41:59.540
at Rutgers, which I much belove,

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:02.450
but which centered Black women's history

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:05.490
from the moment I got there and
was utterly formative to me,

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:07.770
but most history departments, as you know,

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:10.450
even within the U.S.
field, have subdivisions

00:42:10.450 --> 00:42:14.560
that mitigate and constrain any department

00:42:14.560 --> 00:42:17.193
from actually hiring or focusing on race.

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:20.800
- Right, yeah, it's a
very effective mechanism,

00:42:20.800 --> 00:42:22.470
that's what I'm really trying to get at,

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:25.850
is that despite these
incredible interventions, right,

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:28.980
there's been just a
capacity to reproduce itself

00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:30.710
in all of its narrowness.

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:35.120
But let me let Marci jump in, Marci.

00:42:35.120 --> 00:42:36.823
- Yeah, thank you.

00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:43.190
So, yeah, sitting here trying
to think about your question,

00:42:46.120 --> 00:42:49.387
the only word that was going
through my head was like,

00:42:49.387 --> 00:42:51.017
"No," like,

00:42:52.520 --> 00:42:57.093
they're not capable, museums
are not capable, art history,

00:42:58.632 --> 00:43:03.632
for all the reasons we've
been kind of delineating.

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:09.050
And as I tried to kind of
point out in my presentation,

00:43:09.050 --> 00:43:14.050
it's just like this paradox
that I have been grappling with

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:15.300
in doing this work

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:19.950
where it really started when
I was teaching this class

00:43:19.950 --> 00:43:22.917
on Asian-American art when I
first got to Stanford in 2016,

00:43:22.917 --> 00:43:24.650
and I just realized how much of this work

00:43:24.650 --> 00:43:27.053
was in people's homes,

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:29.300
in their garages,

00:43:29.300 --> 00:43:32.760
like, these are works
that have material lives,

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:34.200
they endure in time.

00:43:34.200 --> 00:43:37.440
I mean, Ruth Asawa called those
masks moments of stop time,

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:39.310
which is, on one hand,

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:44.310
I think something that
makes them so powerful,

00:43:45.490 --> 00:43:47.750
and on the other hand,

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:50.700
is what makes them fetish objects, right,

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.620
and people wanna desire them so much.

00:43:53.620 --> 00:43:57.570
And so, like that contradiction
seems to me to be built

00:43:57.570 --> 00:44:02.390
like, directly into the character
of an art object itself.

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:03.223
And, of course, there are

00:44:03.223 --> 00:44:04.390
like kind of contemporary practices

00:44:04.390 --> 00:44:06.820
that attempt to critique
and deconstruct this,

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:10.060
but like, we're talking about

00:44:10.060 --> 00:44:12.970
an entire kind of history of work.

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:13.883
And so,

00:44:18.804 --> 00:44:20.420
I think that for now,

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:22.450
all I've been trying to do is,

00:44:22.450 --> 00:44:24.473
in some ways, tolerate that paradox,

00:44:26.180 --> 00:44:29.837
and know that this work,

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:35.060
the Bernice Bing that I showed you,

00:44:36.220 --> 00:44:38.060
part of the condition of taking it was,

00:44:38.060 --> 00:44:40.100
it was damaged in a fire

00:44:40.100 --> 00:44:44.763
and the museum has the
resources to restore it.

00:44:45.610 --> 00:44:46.810
And she tried to,

00:44:46.810 --> 00:44:48.330
it was important to her as from '65,

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:49.240
and she tried to restore it

00:44:49.240 --> 00:44:51.230
at the end of her life before she passed.

00:44:51.230 --> 00:44:52.693
And so, like,

00:44:54.090 --> 00:44:57.520
those moments I know are important,

00:44:57.520 --> 00:45:00.950
but ultimately, I think the accretion,

00:45:00.950 --> 00:45:04.650
if we're really trying to move
towards like racial justice,

00:45:04.650 --> 00:45:09.270
is that they will destroy the
institutions that they're in.

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:11.780
- Right. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:45:11.780 --> 00:45:13.020
This is one of the tensions.

00:45:13.020 --> 00:45:14.360
Okay, I have one more

00:45:14.360 --> 00:45:18.380
and then I'm gonna move
to our audience questions.

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:20.860
I just wanna encourage
people who are listening

00:45:20.860 --> 00:45:22.960
to place their questions in the Q&amp;A,

00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:25.810
and we'll do our best to get to them.

00:45:25.810 --> 00:45:29.570
One more thing, and I'm
gonna ask Teresa and Noemie

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:33.480
to just sort of begin, but
everyone's welcome to jump in,

00:45:33.480 --> 00:45:35.886
and that is, this really
interesting tension

00:45:35.886 --> 00:45:40.886
between the expression
a number of you used,

00:45:41.680 --> 00:45:45.120
the influence of racial analysis

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:47.920
to force a change in the objects,

00:45:47.920 --> 00:45:50.540
and that by changing the objects,

00:45:50.540 --> 00:45:53.240
we are in the process of doing

00:45:53.240 --> 00:45:56.163
some kind of important
racially transformational work.

00:45:56.163 --> 00:45:58.033
Noemie, you mentioned this.

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:02.471
Teresa, I think you said
something along these lines.

00:46:02.471 --> 00:46:05.360
And by interesting contrast, I think, Rod,

00:46:05.360 --> 00:46:08.360
you were the only one to talk
about sort of new subjects

00:46:08.360 --> 00:46:11.990
in the university coming into place.

00:46:11.990 --> 00:46:13.948
So, and then, of course, Matt,

00:46:13.948 --> 00:46:17.750
the objects that Dana brings back, right,

00:46:17.750 --> 00:46:21.280
are extremely important for her survival.

00:46:21.280 --> 00:46:24.490
So, I'm interested in this
tension around objects,

00:46:24.490 --> 00:46:27.180
the importance of using objects,

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:28.900
but, of course, the objectification,

00:46:28.900 --> 00:46:31.700
the fact that racial
subjects are, in fact,

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:36.700
the original objects, and
what to do with that tension.

00:46:37.290 --> 00:46:39.570
And just talk a little bit more about

00:46:40.450 --> 00:46:44.090
what this changing of objects
means in the disciplines

00:46:44.090 --> 00:46:46.070
and whether or not it must be accompanied

00:46:46.070 --> 00:46:48.450
by a change in subject,

00:46:48.450 --> 00:46:50.823
and how those things might fit together.

00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:57.123
- Teresa, do you wanna go first?

00:46:59.620 --> 00:47:01.563
I'm happy to go, if you prefer.

00:47:03.030 --> 00:47:06.930
- Yeah, I guess I might come
back to this after you again.

00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:09.930
As the sole social scientist here,

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:12.823
I might be coming at this
question a little bit differently.

00:47:14.010 --> 00:47:17.150
Maybe I'll just answer
this by way of explaining

00:47:17.150 --> 00:47:21.414
my own desire, impulse, to
even go into anthropology.

00:47:21.414 --> 00:47:22.420
As many of you may know,

00:47:22.420 --> 00:47:24.190
I mean, anthropology was a discipline

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:27.280
that relied extensively
upon the study of others,

00:47:27.280 --> 00:47:29.830
of Native Americans, of our bones,

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:32.320
our literal bodies, right?

00:47:32.320 --> 00:47:36.460
And this also fields, museum collections,

00:47:36.460 --> 00:47:37.970
early ethnological collection.

00:47:37.970 --> 00:47:41.900
So, my interest is really
around the material culture

00:47:41.900 --> 00:47:43.950
and the actual objects, right?

00:47:43.950 --> 00:47:47.640
So, my initial desire
to go into anthropology

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:49.160
was through museum anthropology.

00:47:49.160 --> 00:47:51.880
I wanted to be in these museum spaces

00:47:51.880 --> 00:47:56.880
to try to curate this history
in a more appropriate way,

00:47:56.890 --> 00:47:58.780
in a way that was more respectful

00:47:58.780 --> 00:48:01.980
to my ancestors and our relatives

00:48:01.980 --> 00:48:05.010
that are literally still
in use museum collections.

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:08.930
And like all of you that
have expressed this caution

00:48:08.930 --> 00:48:12.240
around the potential for
institutional change,

00:48:12.240 --> 00:48:14.560
I felt that very strongly
actually with museums.

00:48:14.560 --> 00:48:18.430
And it actually was part
of the reason why I've,

00:48:18.430 --> 00:48:20.220
even though I continue to do museum work

00:48:20.220 --> 00:48:25.220
and I continue to
guest-curate for exhibitions,

00:48:27.050 --> 00:48:29.670
I still found potential
in education itself.

00:48:29.670 --> 00:48:31.980
So, this is why I've
gone through this route

00:48:31.980 --> 00:48:34.290
of doing research and education,

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:37.220
rather than going the route
of becoming a curator.

00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:38.420
That being said,

00:48:38.420 --> 00:48:43.400
there's still a lot to be
changed within anthropology.

00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:45.010
At the University of Chicago,

00:48:45.010 --> 00:48:47.440
I'm one of just a few Native faculty

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:50.658
who have ever existed, (chuckles)

00:48:50.658 --> 00:48:53.790
and there is no existing
Native studies program.

00:48:55.200 --> 00:48:57.240
When I was hired, and when I accepted,

00:48:57.240 --> 00:49:00.820
it was on the condition
that there would be

00:49:00.820 --> 00:49:03.543
this Native studies program information.

00:49:04.980 --> 00:49:07.250
I know there's these
conversations happening

00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:10.280
and there's definitely some movement,

00:49:10.280 --> 00:49:11.620
but I know that this will take time,

00:49:11.620 --> 00:49:13.513
institutional change takes time.

00:49:15.676 --> 00:49:20.676
So, for myself, where I see
maybe the potential for change,

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:23.210
in what I think all of us

00:49:23.210 --> 00:49:25.150
maybe should be pushing back against

00:49:25.150 --> 00:49:26.610
across each of our disciplines,

00:49:26.610 --> 00:49:28.200
is just the single author model.

00:49:28.200 --> 00:49:29.033
Like, that's something

00:49:29.033 --> 00:49:31.320
that has become increasingly
bothersome to me

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:34.570
that I didn't really question
at all as a graduate student.

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:37.310
And I've had these conversations
with a lot of other folks.

00:49:37.310 --> 00:49:38.830
So, now that I'm in my post-doc,

00:49:38.830 --> 00:49:41.850
I've found myself actually
increasingly collaborating

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:46.630
with people in other
disciplines, in the sciences,

00:49:46.630 --> 00:49:50.020
in public health, or folks
who are working in hydrology,

00:49:50.020 --> 00:49:52.150
like things I didn't
really imagine before,

00:49:52.150 --> 00:49:54.410
but it was because the problems

00:49:54.410 --> 00:49:55.860
that I'm confronting in my research,

00:49:55.860 --> 00:49:58.847
I think, necessitates people who are doing

00:49:58.847 --> 00:50:00.730
the like on-the-ground work,

00:50:00.730 --> 00:50:02.910
and I can bring like a historical,

00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:06.520
or a political sort of
analysis to that sort of work.

00:50:06.520 --> 00:50:08.590
And I work on environmental contamination

00:50:08.590 --> 00:50:11.173
and Indigenous sovereignty
in the Southwest.

00:50:13.420 --> 00:50:17.180
So, those are kind of some of my concerns

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:21.160
that I've been struggling with
over the past year or two,

00:50:21.160 --> 00:50:25.000
is I'm now charting this new
trajectory of my research

00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:26.580
and, obviously, COVID has thrown

00:50:26.580 --> 00:50:30.933
a lot of my previous plans ajar a bit.

00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:34.810
But yeah, I would love to
hear from the rest of you

00:50:34.810 --> 00:50:38.670
about what, not just
within our own disciplines

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:42.190
and departments, but the
larger structures of academia,

00:50:42.190 --> 00:50:46.460
and how we can imagine change

00:50:46.460 --> 00:50:51.460
or ways to push back on these structures

00:50:51.900 --> 00:50:55.503
that have been confining to us.

00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:04.250
- Yeah, so, I'm going to
answer your question, Tricia,

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:06.270
about subjects versus objects,

00:51:06.270 --> 00:51:08.490
although there is no versus really,

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:09.980
but just, I wanted to go back first

00:51:09.980 --> 00:51:11.760
to the question about disciplines,

00:51:11.760 --> 00:51:15.420
can the disciplines
help, are they capable?

00:51:15.420 --> 00:51:18.640
I will say that I personally
have very little interest

00:51:18.640 --> 00:51:20.470
in disciplinarity, or disciplines,

00:51:20.470 --> 00:51:23.530
or the survival or
capabilities of discipline.

00:51:23.530 --> 00:51:25.800
I got my PhD in theater

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:28.570
from a Department of English
and Comparative Literature,

00:51:28.570 --> 00:51:31.430
and it was working mostly
on Spanish and French lit

00:51:31.430 --> 00:51:32.450
within that structure.

00:51:32.450 --> 00:51:33.840
So, I think it's
particularly the case for me,

00:51:33.840 --> 00:51:35.400
but it's the case of all of us

00:51:35.400 --> 00:51:36.870
that we are not working in a discipline,

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:39.800
we're all working within a Venn diagram

00:51:39.800 --> 00:51:42.510
of multiple disciplines, right?

00:51:42.510 --> 00:51:43.790
And so, I would just say

00:51:43.790 --> 00:51:47.700
that I am very opportunistic about this.

00:51:47.700 --> 00:51:50.140
I don't have faith in the disciplines,

00:51:50.140 --> 00:51:54.040
but I also am very willing to
mobilize them strategically.

00:51:54.040 --> 00:51:54.940
And for instance,

00:51:54.940 --> 00:51:57.930
right now I'm teaching a course
called Black Shakespeare,

00:51:57.930 --> 00:52:00.630
and I am very willing, very ready,

00:52:00.630 --> 00:52:05.140
to harness all the disciplinary
affordances of English,

00:52:05.140 --> 00:52:07.730
studying the hyper-canonical
figure of Shakespeare

00:52:07.730 --> 00:52:09.500
if it allows me to get students

00:52:09.500 --> 00:52:11.180
to think about race critically,

00:52:11.180 --> 00:52:13.830
to bring into the room all
the founders of the field,

00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:16.150
and thinkers in Black studies.

00:52:16.150 --> 00:52:20.327
And I think a number of
us have been doing that.

00:52:20.327 --> 00:52:23.548
And I'm thinking about the involvement

00:52:23.548 --> 00:52:25.100
of major institutions,

00:52:25.100 --> 00:52:27.060
like the Shakespeare
Association of America,

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:29.820
which has been a force of
transformation in the field

00:52:29.820 --> 00:52:31.305
over the last couple of years.

00:52:31.305 --> 00:52:34.730
So, I don't want to sound too naive here.

00:52:34.730 --> 00:52:38.670
I think I'm in line with everybody here,

00:52:38.670 --> 00:52:42.690
but I am very willing to be opportunistic

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:46.600
as far as disciplines are
concerned to make change happen.

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:50.090
But concerning the objects
versus subject difference,

00:52:50.090 --> 00:52:54.880
perhaps I wasn't clear, I was
actually trying to suggest

00:52:54.880 --> 00:52:58.320
that this involvement

00:52:58.320 --> 00:53:01.460
of my field in building a pipeline

00:53:01.460 --> 00:53:03.740
to diversify the professorate

00:53:03.740 --> 00:53:07.870
means that we are very acutely,
painfully, acutely aware,

00:53:07.870 --> 00:53:10.700
of the fact that we need new
subjects doing this work.

00:53:10.700 --> 00:53:13.900
As I was mentioning, there are
less than 20 faculty members

00:53:13.900 --> 00:53:16.770
who are Black Shakespeareans
in this country, right?

00:53:16.770 --> 00:53:19.590
Every department, every,
every, every English major

00:53:19.590 --> 00:53:21.690
is going to take a Shakespeare
course at some point.

00:53:21.690 --> 00:53:24.570
20 professors are Black, doing this work.

00:53:24.570 --> 00:53:26.300
So, we are very aware of it.

00:53:26.300 --> 00:53:31.300
But to clarify what I meant
when I said new objects,

00:53:31.470 --> 00:53:33.760
I don't mean that the objects
themselves are changing,

00:53:33.760 --> 00:53:36.900
I mean that if you bring new
subjects to work on them,

00:53:36.900 --> 00:53:39.830
they will see new things because
the way we read the world

00:53:39.830 --> 00:53:42.120
is positioned and conditional

00:53:42.120 --> 00:53:44.490
upon our own positionality, right?

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:48.850
So, if you get a scholar
like me, for instance,

00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:50.360
who lives in a Black body

00:53:50.360 --> 00:53:53.080
and is used to speaking with an accent,

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:55.690
then the question of the Black accents

00:53:55.690 --> 00:53:57.210
in the early modern repertoire

00:53:57.210 --> 00:53:59.100
is going to be something that they see

00:53:59.100 --> 00:54:01.930
in plays that other scholars
had not seen before.

00:54:01.930 --> 00:54:04.570
So, I think actually that the production

00:54:04.570 --> 00:54:07.490
of new objects of
knowledge and new subjects,

00:54:07.490 --> 00:54:10.960
those are actually completely
embedded, they work together.

00:54:10.960 --> 00:54:12.662
And this is the only way, really,

00:54:12.662 --> 00:54:14.900
to see new exciting things

00:54:14.900 --> 00:54:17.753
about the figure of like
Shakespeare at this point.

00:54:19.790 --> 00:54:21.150
- Marci, or Matt, or Rod,

00:54:21.150 --> 00:54:26.150
any things emerge from this
that you want to weigh on?

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:28.760
Yeah, go ahead, Matt.

00:54:30.090 --> 00:54:34.550
- I just wanna say what
Noemie and Teresa were saying

00:54:34.550 --> 00:54:37.820
is so totally and devastatingly apparent

00:54:37.820 --> 00:54:40.190
that if you don't diversify the faculty

00:54:40.190 --> 00:54:44.210
at the same time that you
diversify the material we study,

00:54:44.210 --> 00:54:47.470
you end up with the
story of the moo-h family

00:54:47.470 --> 00:54:50.030
and the story of Penn and Princeton,

00:54:50.030 --> 00:54:52.460
which is currently all over the news,

00:54:52.460 --> 00:54:55.000
which is a repeat of the story
that Teresa started with,

00:54:55.000 --> 00:54:59.850
the ways in which the pain

00:54:59.850 --> 00:55:01.700
of minoritized subjects

00:55:01.700 --> 00:55:05.416
becomes the object of
established disciplines,

00:55:05.416 --> 00:55:09.293
is just too familiar
and can't be repeated.

00:55:11.827 --> 00:55:13.140
- And I'll add that

00:55:15.772 --> 00:55:17.390
this is social movement wisdom

00:55:18.240 --> 00:55:23.240
in terms of the link
between the constitution

00:55:24.270 --> 00:55:26.370
of the object

00:55:26.370 --> 00:55:30.790
and the simultaneous
introduction of new subjects.

00:55:30.790 --> 00:55:35.230
if you think about, for
instance, what Angela Davis says

00:55:35.230 --> 00:55:39.230
in that documentary,
"Herbert's Hippopotamus"

00:55:39.230 --> 00:55:43.260
about Herbert Marcuse at UC San Diego,

00:55:43.260 --> 00:55:47.110
she's talking about the
Lumumba-Zapata Movement in 1969,

00:55:47.110 --> 00:55:49.440
when the University says,
"We're gonna have a new college.

00:55:49.440 --> 00:55:50.770
It's gonna be called Third College."

00:55:50.770 --> 00:55:53.570
And she says, "No, my
comrades and I have decided

00:55:53.570 --> 00:55:57.410
it will be called
Lumumba-Zapata College. (laughs)

00:55:57.410 --> 00:55:59.400
It will not be called third college.

00:55:59.400 --> 00:56:02.220
And we had a curriculum," right?

00:56:02.220 --> 00:56:06.973
So, they took over the
college with the intention

00:56:07.920 --> 00:56:11.270
of producing and introducing new subjects,

00:56:11.270 --> 00:56:14.010
peoples, communities, into the college,

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:16.140
and they had a curriculum, right,

00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:21.140
so that the introduction of
these folks necessarily meant

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:25.803
an epistemic shift,

00:56:26.750 --> 00:56:31.750
that there had to be a new way
of understanding the world,

00:56:31.940 --> 00:56:36.450
the U.S., race, colonialism,
the legacies of colonialism.

00:56:36.450 --> 00:56:38.960
And we see that kinda thing
repeated, for instance,

00:56:38.960 --> 00:56:43.080
at the Open Admissions
Movement at City College,

00:56:43.080 --> 00:56:45.290
that the introduction, in that context,

00:56:45.290 --> 00:56:49.193
of African-Americans and
Puerto Rican students,

00:56:50.230 --> 00:56:54.440
anybody who wanted to be in
could get in, was the idea,

00:56:54.440 --> 00:56:57.380
they also produced a new curriculum

00:56:57.380 --> 00:56:58.770
and had a vision for a school,

00:56:58.770 --> 00:57:00.590
the same thing for San Francisco State.

00:57:00.590 --> 00:57:05.590
So, the link between the
constitution of epistemic objects

00:57:06.270 --> 00:57:09.130
and the production of new subjects,

00:57:09.130 --> 00:57:11.143
that's social movement wisdom.

00:57:12.610 --> 00:57:13.993
- Yeah, yeah. Marci?

00:57:15.010 --> 00:57:17.689
- So, you mentioned San
Francisco State, Rod.

00:57:17.689 --> 00:57:22.689
Actually, I was doing some
work with an artist actually,

00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:27.830
who has made work about the
Third World Liberation Front

00:57:27.830 --> 00:57:32.020
and he pulled out an archival
document he had in his studio

00:57:32.020 --> 00:57:34.380
'cause he was like
hanging out with everyone,

00:57:34.380 --> 00:57:37.680
and one of the things on
the third world curriculum,

00:57:37.680 --> 00:57:42.240
like in '68, was an
Asian-American art course.

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:44.277
And so, there's that.

00:57:44.277 --> 00:57:47.800
But I just wanna say I agree
with what everyone has said,

00:57:47.800 --> 00:57:50.170
of course, about new subjects

00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:53.293
and new objects being kind of intertwined.

00:57:56.300 --> 00:58:00.620
When I first drafted my talk,
my remarks were way too long,

00:58:00.620 --> 00:58:02.100
but I had this story about like,

00:58:02.100 --> 00:58:04.210
which I'm sure all of
us have similar stories,

00:58:04.210 --> 00:58:07.950
which is like, like, in 2014,

00:58:07.950 --> 00:58:09.580
so, this was like seven years ago,

00:58:09.580 --> 00:58:10.920
and I know it was the state

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:15.480
because it was around
the time of Ferguson,

00:58:15.480 --> 00:58:19.460
I went to someone on faculty

00:58:19.460 --> 00:58:21.870
at the Institute of Fine Arts at NYU,

00:58:21.870 --> 00:58:23.903
where I was getting my PhD in art history.

00:58:23.903 --> 00:58:27.090
It's no one on my committee,
or not my advisor,

00:58:27.090 --> 00:58:29.210
and told him I wanted to work on race

00:58:29.210 --> 00:58:31.820
and Asian-American things.

00:58:31.820 --> 00:58:36.820
And he told me that I
should work on Jewishness

00:58:37.070 --> 00:58:40.611
because art historians are Jewish,

00:58:40.611 --> 00:58:43.702
and so, they'll be more
sympathetic to my topic.

00:58:43.702 --> 00:58:46.430
This is like seven years ago. (laughs)

00:58:46.430 --> 00:58:49.350
And I think that all of the assumptions

00:58:50.680 --> 00:58:53.563
about the relationship
of subjects and objects

00:58:53.563 --> 00:58:55.513
is in that statement.

00:58:57.670 --> 00:59:00.313
- Yeah, you say seven years ago,

00:59:01.150 --> 00:59:03.000
much of that meant it was a long time ago

00:59:03.000 --> 00:59:04.290
or it was just yesterday,

00:59:04.290 --> 00:59:06.800
that's how horrible the thought is.

00:59:06.800 --> 00:59:10.090
- I have no idea honestly, but-

00:59:10.090 --> 00:59:14.570
- It almost feels like a 1958
kind of comment, yeah, yeah.

00:59:14.570 --> 00:59:17.510
Time flies and then it
doesn't move at all.

00:59:17.510 --> 00:59:18.690
- Yeah, yeah.

00:59:18.690 --> 00:59:23.690
And so, it's just to say that
art history is so conservative

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:28.960
and has been for such a long time.

00:59:28.960 --> 00:59:30.083
It's sometimes even really
hard to explain this

00:59:30.083 --> 00:59:31.190
to my friends in English.

00:59:31.190 --> 00:59:34.160
They were like there's a subfield
of Asian-American studies,

00:59:34.160 --> 00:59:36.710
there's no Asian-American literature.

00:59:36.710 --> 00:59:38.660
Some field doesn't exist in art history.

00:59:38.660 --> 00:59:40.230
And I think the other thing

00:59:40.230 --> 00:59:42.380
about like new objects and the pipeline,

00:59:42.380 --> 00:59:44.610
something that Teresa mentioned as well,

00:59:44.610 --> 00:59:46.950
it's like, I talk about this
with my friends of color

00:59:46.950 --> 00:59:48.950
who are art historians, all the time,

00:59:48.950 --> 00:59:51.810
and we're like, it's the privilege

00:59:51.810 --> 00:59:54.260
of being able to work in
a museum over the summer

00:59:55.200 --> 00:59:58.633
and get that hands-on
experience with objects,

00:59:59.540 --> 01:00:02.760
which then gives you connections
to go into fellowships.

01:00:02.760 --> 01:00:06.460
And so, academia is
actually really interleaved

01:00:06.460 --> 01:00:10.910
with a lot of like kind
of funding organizations

01:00:10.910 --> 01:00:13.023
that themselves still are,

01:00:14.570 --> 01:00:18.520
yeah, like, are still
kinda guided by capital.

01:00:18.520 --> 01:00:23.520
And so, there's a lot of
work to be done, still.

01:00:24.370 --> 01:00:27.210
- Right, I mean, and this connects,

01:00:27.210 --> 01:00:30.700
what you're saying,
Marci, to a broader issue,

01:00:30.700 --> 01:00:32.950
I'm gonna just throw it out
there while it's on my mind,

01:00:32.950 --> 01:00:35.890
but I do wanna start asking
some audience questions,

01:00:35.890 --> 01:00:38.160
and there's a really
good one to start with,

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:41.640
but there's this realization

01:00:41.640 --> 01:00:46.580
that the work that has
to be done is really,

01:00:46.580 --> 01:00:50.400
the academic work is tethered
to structures of power

01:00:51.760 --> 01:00:53.880
that our departments, disciplines,

01:00:53.880 --> 01:00:55.950
and even some of our inter-disciplines,

01:00:55.950 --> 01:00:58.270
reinforce through a sense of hierarchy

01:00:58.270 --> 01:01:02.960
about what kinds of work
matters, how that work is done,

01:01:02.960 --> 01:01:06.920
and who's going to evaluate
the value of that work.

01:01:06.920 --> 01:01:10.624
And just, I mean, Noemie,
I agree that English

01:01:10.624 --> 01:01:12.913
is a little more porous,

01:01:14.330 --> 01:01:19.330
but I wouldn't say that the
level of creative integration

01:01:19.670 --> 01:01:21.590
and complexity that you're doing

01:01:21.590 --> 01:01:24.313
is pretty standard in English departments.

01:01:26.779 --> 01:01:29.490
That's the problem with
gathering creative people

01:01:29.490 --> 01:01:31.130
who are pushing all these boundaries

01:01:31.130 --> 01:01:33.160
because they're like,
"Hey, yeah, look at us.

01:01:33.160 --> 01:01:34.000
We're here." And I'm like, "Yeah."

01:01:34.000 --> 01:01:36.030
And then there's like 40 million people

01:01:36.030 --> 01:01:37.770
standing over there in the corner

01:01:37.770 --> 01:01:40.020
in a straight disciplinary line.

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:41.420
So, anyway, I won't go back to that

01:01:41.420 --> 01:01:42.600
because that's my pet peeve.

01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:46.263
And this is a question for
anyone who wants to answer it,

01:01:47.280 --> 01:01:52.250
but it's drawn on Matt's concept
and part of what he said.

01:01:52.250 --> 01:01:53.520
So, I'm gonna read the question

01:01:53.520 --> 01:01:57.000
and give you all a chance
to answer it, okay.

01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:58.857
This person is asking the following.

01:01:58.857 --> 01:02:01.380
"I'm moved by Doctor Guterl's call

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:03.750
for us to prepare for a reckoning

01:02:03.750 --> 01:02:08.630
that stands to be cataclysmic
to the academy as we know it.

01:02:08.630 --> 01:02:11.730
I wanna ask the Roundtable
what their knives

01:02:11.730 --> 01:02:15.170
and their emergency packs are.

01:02:15.170 --> 01:02:17.650
Given that this reckoning
is not just coming,

01:02:17.650 --> 01:02:19.430
but for graduate students,

01:02:19.430 --> 01:02:23.240
postdocs, and adjuncts
among us, it's already here,

01:02:23.240 --> 01:02:26.370
what are the contingency
plans and alternative homes

01:02:26.370 --> 01:02:29.440
for intellectual work that
we should be investing in?"

01:02:29.440 --> 01:02:30.720
So, there are two questions there.

01:02:30.720 --> 01:02:32.830
I will just ask that you take one

01:02:32.830 --> 01:02:34.240
and that will just give us room

01:02:34.240 --> 01:02:35.720
for other people to take another.

01:02:35.720 --> 01:02:40.710
So, one is what are the
knives and emergency packs

01:02:42.541 --> 01:02:44.190
to prepare for the reckoning

01:02:44.190 --> 01:02:46.910
that stands to be
cataclysmic in the academy?

01:02:46.910 --> 01:02:51.630
That's Question A, so, what's
your emergency pack/knife?

01:02:51.630 --> 01:02:52.463
And the other is,

01:02:52.463 --> 01:02:54.790
given that the reckoning
is basically here,

01:02:54.790 --> 01:02:57.720
what are the contingency
plans and alternative homes

01:02:57.720 --> 01:03:00.337
for intellectual work that
we should be investing in?

01:03:00.337 --> 01:03:02.730
And this kind of dovetails
with what Rod was saying

01:03:02.730 --> 01:03:04.940
about having your own curriculum, right,

01:03:04.940 --> 01:03:07.313
and, as a possibility
now, where that might be,

01:03:07.313 --> 01:03:09.040
you might have other locations.

01:03:09.040 --> 01:03:12.500
But I will open the floor to
anyone who wants to pick up one

01:03:12.500 --> 01:03:14.657
or the other of those questions.

01:03:20.730 --> 01:03:23.550
- Well, I'm happy to get us started

01:03:23.550 --> 01:03:27.860
with the second question, plan
Bs, what are some plan Bs?

01:03:27.860 --> 01:03:29.290
I think the plan Bs are very compatible

01:03:29.290 --> 01:03:30.920
with the plan As to start with.

01:03:30.920 --> 01:03:34.283
But my point is that I am thinking,

01:03:35.420 --> 01:03:39.630
every kind of work that will
allow the transformation

01:03:39.630 --> 01:03:41.150
that we're trying to have happen

01:03:41.150 --> 01:03:45.320
in the Plan A in the academy,
every single form of work

01:03:45.320 --> 01:03:47.870
that will enable that to radiate, right?

01:03:47.870 --> 01:03:51.820
For instance, we want
students to be, in my field,

01:03:51.820 --> 01:03:55.310
already invested, having
some interest in working

01:03:55.310 --> 01:03:58.040
in pre-modern race
studies, dealing with race,

01:03:58.040 --> 01:04:00.120
and Shakespeare, and how
everything is working.

01:04:00.120 --> 01:04:01.750
It's already too late, usually,

01:04:01.750 --> 01:04:03.480
when they arrive in college level.

01:04:03.480 --> 01:04:05.070
They already think that Shakespeare

01:04:05.070 --> 01:04:07.740
and everything from that
time period is not for them.

01:04:07.740 --> 01:04:11.300
So, actually, investing
heavily in the training

01:04:11.300 --> 01:04:13.650
of our high school teachers
and giving them the tools

01:04:13.650 --> 01:04:16.800
to do some of that work earlier
on when it's still possible,

01:04:16.800 --> 01:04:18.210
that is really important.

01:04:18.210 --> 01:04:22.060
I'm also thinking about the kind of work

01:04:22.060 --> 01:04:25.110
that we see partner institutions doing,

01:04:25.110 --> 01:04:28.720
for instance, again, I'm
thinking about what I know,

01:04:28.720 --> 01:04:31.280
I'm thinking about the
institutions that I'm working with,

01:04:31.280 --> 01:04:33.228
I'm thinking of the Newberry
Library, for instance,

01:04:33.228 --> 01:04:35.770
which is, at the moment, really invested

01:04:35.770 --> 01:04:37.290
in early modern critical race studies,

01:04:37.290 --> 01:04:40.830
and is trying to help
make that work visible.

01:04:40.830 --> 01:04:44.100
So, in 2023 RaceB4Race
is coming to Chicago.

01:04:44.100 --> 01:04:47.030
The Newberry Library is
curating an exhibition

01:04:47.030 --> 01:04:49.170
that will be opened to everybody,

01:04:49.170 --> 01:04:51.270
to the Republic, and to
high school students,

01:04:51.270 --> 01:04:54.600
in order to familiarize
folks with those questions.

01:04:54.600 --> 01:04:58.100
There are jobs there, there
are forms of expertise

01:04:58.100 --> 01:04:59.880
that we are trained to provide,

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:01.990
that we are trained to
give to our own students

01:05:01.990 --> 01:05:05.610
that were graduate students,
and that we don't have the time

01:05:05.610 --> 01:05:07.930
or the bandwidth to share with everybody.

01:05:07.930 --> 01:05:11.390
So, involving the excellent students

01:05:11.390 --> 01:05:14.860
that we train into that work
so that they might be able

01:05:14.860 --> 01:05:18.840
to share that knowledge
as their contingency plan,

01:05:18.840 --> 01:05:21.520
I think, is something that
should be on the table.

01:05:21.520 --> 01:05:25.180
- Hmm, very helpful.
Excellent way to get us going.

01:05:25.180 --> 01:05:29.210
Anybody else wanna jump in on Plan B

01:05:29.210 --> 01:05:32.770
or other intellectual work
preparing for the reckoning,

01:05:32.770 --> 01:05:34.853
contingency, alternative home?

01:05:35.860 --> 01:05:38.770
- Well, I'll just add a little
bit to what you were saying

01:05:39.670 --> 01:05:40.940
since I'm also in Chicago

01:05:40.940 --> 01:05:45.520
and I am doing projects
with Newberry right now.

01:05:45.520 --> 01:05:48.720
I think a lot of my public
scholarship, as I said,

01:05:48.720 --> 01:05:50.030
I started in museum studies

01:05:50.030 --> 01:05:54.107
and even though I didn't
go the full curation route,

01:05:54.107 --> 01:05:56.240
I've maintained a foot in that world

01:05:56.240 --> 01:05:58.680
because of its importance
for public education,

01:05:58.680 --> 01:06:01.540
because of the failure to prepare students

01:06:02.870 --> 01:06:06.480
having any sort of
understanding of Native history,

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:09.890
usually in their K-through-12 education.

01:06:09.890 --> 01:06:13.150
And then once I get freshmen
or sophomore students,

01:06:13.150 --> 01:06:16.040
and having to really start
from the very beginning

01:06:16.040 --> 01:06:18.610
with Native history before I can even get

01:06:18.610 --> 01:06:22.590
into more complicated
ideas around sovereignty,

01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:25.850
or land status, or blood quantum,

01:06:25.850 --> 01:06:30.820
I think one way to do
that is through museums,

01:06:30.820 --> 01:06:33.900
because this is a place that
a lot of students will go,

01:06:33.900 --> 01:06:35.650
and that is their first introduction

01:06:35.650 --> 01:06:36.950
to what Native people are.

01:06:38.610 --> 01:06:41.020
As somebody who also got my degree at NYU,

01:06:41.020 --> 01:06:44.030
I'd go to the American
museum of natural history,

01:06:44.030 --> 01:06:48.557
and it's appalling, as most
museums of natural history are,

01:06:48.557 --> 01:06:51.910
and you literally have all of
the other people of the world,

01:06:51.910 --> 01:06:55.080
you have the Indigenous
people, you have Africans,

01:06:55.080 --> 01:06:57.340
you have everyone that is not White,

01:06:57.340 --> 01:07:01.550
in a museum on display next to-

01:07:01.550 --> 01:07:04.320
- Bear.
- Wooly mammoths. (chuckles)

01:07:04.320 --> 01:07:07.510
So, it's part of this
continuum and hierarchy

01:07:08.960 --> 01:07:10.653
that, I think,

01:07:11.890 --> 01:07:15.863
children begin to
understand and naturalize.

01:07:16.960 --> 01:07:21.200
With that being said, so, the
field museum here in Chicago

01:07:21.200 --> 01:07:23.560
also has a really bad history.

01:07:23.560 --> 01:07:27.913
Its earliest collections are
through the 1893 World's Fair.

01:07:29.200 --> 01:07:30.700
And they also, until recently,

01:07:30.700 --> 01:07:34.090
had old Native-American displays.

01:07:34.090 --> 01:07:36.660
But for the past two years,

01:07:36.660 --> 01:07:39.720
the institution has
undertaken this new initiative

01:07:39.720 --> 01:07:42.010
to redesign their
Native-American galleries

01:07:42.010 --> 01:07:46.010
and to invite Native scholars, curators,

01:07:46.010 --> 01:07:50.170
to help with this new formation.

01:07:50.170 --> 01:07:52.200
So, I was invited to be a guest curator

01:07:52.200 --> 01:07:53.380
on some of the cases,

01:07:53.380 --> 01:07:55.800
and I've been working on
this for the past year,

01:07:55.800 --> 01:07:56.833
and this has really,

01:07:57.900 --> 01:08:00.550
there's been some challenges
along the way, I won't lie,

01:08:00.550 --> 01:08:04.960
but having the opportunity to
actually help guide the voice

01:08:04.960 --> 01:08:08.010
and interpretation of what
is going to be on display now

01:08:08.010 --> 01:08:11.587
for the next however it does in 20 years,

01:08:11.587 --> 01:08:15.070
like, that to me, is
possibly more impactful

01:08:15.070 --> 01:08:18.070
than any like journal article
that I'm writing right now,

01:08:18.070 --> 01:08:19.753
in terms of audience reach.

01:08:21.400 --> 01:08:24.680
And those sorts of things

01:08:26.604 --> 01:08:28.730
aren't necessarily the first thing

01:08:28.730 --> 01:08:32.450
that one considers for their tenure file.

01:08:32.450 --> 01:08:34.220
But for me, what's important

01:08:34.220 --> 01:08:38.040
is having my scholarship reach places

01:08:38.040 --> 01:08:40.290
that's not behind a paywall.

01:08:40.290 --> 01:08:43.490
So, just building off of
what all of you were saying

01:08:43.490 --> 01:08:45.530
is, like, these are like tangible ways

01:08:45.530 --> 01:08:48.720
that we can try to shift discourses

01:08:48.720 --> 01:08:50.620
and to educate people in ways

01:08:50.620 --> 01:08:53.721
that perhaps aren't necessarily

01:08:53.721 --> 01:08:55.921
the traditional ways
that we've been taught.

01:08:57.290 --> 01:08:59.090
So, yeah, I think museum institutions

01:08:59.090 --> 01:09:01.033
is one way to go about that change.

01:09:02.593 --> 01:09:04.460
- [Tricia] Thank you.

01:09:04.460 --> 01:09:05.503
- I can jump in.

01:09:07.120 --> 01:09:11.193
So, for me, this answer,
like, A and B are related.

01:09:12.460 --> 01:09:15.100
This past year, I have a lot of friends

01:09:15.100 --> 01:09:18.790
who are faculty members of
color, especially junior faculty,

01:09:18.790 --> 01:09:22.350
and I don't think I've talked
to a single one of them

01:09:22.350 --> 01:09:25.493
without the topic of leaving
the academy coming up.

01:09:26.960 --> 01:09:30.683
I think all of us are
just like, "What do we do?

01:09:32.100 --> 01:09:33.750
And how is our labor being used?"

01:09:35.610 --> 01:09:39.760
And actually, that might be the answer,

01:09:39.760 --> 01:09:42.910
which is, not necessarily leaving,

01:09:42.910 --> 01:09:44.260
but being willing to leave.

01:09:45.790 --> 01:09:47.863
And realizing that,

01:09:49.040 --> 01:09:51.783
well, I mean, one, being in
conversation with people,

01:09:53.220 --> 01:09:55.350
so, being in community with people

01:09:55.350 --> 01:09:59.140
who are engaging with similar things,

01:09:59.140 --> 01:10:04.060
but two, like, I'd say like
my toolkit is psychoanalysis,

01:10:04.060 --> 01:10:07.340
because I've really had to
think very hard this year

01:10:07.340 --> 01:10:09.570
about my own attachments,

01:10:09.570 --> 01:10:11.550
my continued attachments to Whiteness,

01:10:11.550 --> 01:10:15.993
like structures of Whiteness
and structures of legitimacy,

01:10:20.516 --> 01:10:24.650
and, of course, that work is
forever incomplete, right?

01:10:24.650 --> 01:10:26.380
But I really think that

01:10:28.010 --> 01:10:31.200
thinking about my own attachments

01:10:32.290 --> 01:10:36.390
and being willing to see
outside of the structure

01:10:36.390 --> 01:10:38.413
and realize that, like,

01:10:40.080 --> 01:10:43.560
I am a whole person without it,

01:10:43.560 --> 01:10:47.570
is, to me, the first
step in trying to rethink

01:10:47.570 --> 01:10:48.720
what I need to rethink.

01:10:53.579 --> 01:10:56.140
- I think there have to be
a number of different ways

01:10:56.140 --> 01:10:58.330
to approach this, and I think we need

01:10:58.330 --> 01:11:01.320
all of these kinds of modes.

01:11:01.320 --> 01:11:05.390
I, for one, Tricia, you
know as well as I do

01:11:05.390 --> 01:11:07.353
that giving up on the institution,

01:11:08.250 --> 01:11:10.400
all of us giving up on
the institution at once

01:11:10.400 --> 01:11:14.130
would be destructive

01:11:14.130 --> 01:11:16.580
and catastrophic in the
ways that I'm describing.

01:11:16.580 --> 01:11:17.413
And in fact, higher education

01:11:17.413 --> 01:11:20.750
is one of the least innovative industries

01:11:20.750 --> 01:11:21.583
or business models.

01:11:21.583 --> 01:11:22.416
If you think about it,

01:11:22.416 --> 01:11:24.840
we've seen very few new
university startups,

01:11:24.840 --> 01:11:27.970
and it's amazing how
durable these brands are

01:11:27.970 --> 01:11:32.330
and how resistant they are to destruction.

01:11:32.330 --> 01:11:33.920
We haven't seen the university collapses

01:11:33.920 --> 01:11:35.520
that we thought we were gonna see

01:11:35.520 --> 01:11:38.720
at the start of this pandemic,
and we're not likely to.

01:11:38.720 --> 01:11:40.960
So, one thing I would suggest

01:11:40.960 --> 01:11:44.740
is that new ways of thinking
about the institution

01:11:44.740 --> 01:11:48.000
could be materialized in front of us.

01:11:48.000 --> 01:11:51.890
And one exit strategy for us
all would be to think about

01:11:51.890 --> 01:11:53.980
whether the brands we attach ourselves to,

01:11:53.980 --> 01:11:56.480
Brown, Yale, University
of Chicago, and Stanford,

01:11:56.480 --> 01:11:58.470
are the brands we wanna be attached to,

01:11:58.470 --> 01:11:59.880
whether we wanna be attached

01:11:59.880 --> 01:12:01.010
to a different kind of community

01:12:01.010 --> 01:12:02.960
and a different kind of institution

01:12:02.960 --> 01:12:04.263
that we might make anew.

01:12:05.440 --> 01:12:09.927
At the same time, like, I also
know that, like, sometimes

01:12:10.880 --> 01:12:13.010
the difference between
hiring a person at Brown

01:12:13.010 --> 01:12:15.030
is the difference between one last email

01:12:15.030 --> 01:12:17.410
in the middle of the night,
one last cup of coffee,

01:12:17.410 --> 01:12:20.190
one last meeting with
the Dean, one last try,

01:12:20.190 --> 01:12:22.180
and you can't stop that, right?

01:12:22.180 --> 01:12:25.200
So, as a senior faculty
member, I can't stop that labor

01:12:25.200 --> 01:12:27.520
because that could be the difference

01:12:27.520 --> 01:12:29.370
between the right person getting the job

01:12:29.370 --> 01:12:30.970
and the wrong person getting the job.

01:12:30.970 --> 01:12:35.093
- Right, and that could have
a 40-year catastrophic impact.

01:12:37.080 --> 01:12:38.960
Yeah, sorry, I didn't mean to interrupt,

01:12:38.960 --> 01:12:40.480
I just I couldn't help

01:12:40.480 --> 01:12:44.203
but then draw out the consequences, right?

01:12:45.081 --> 01:12:47.670
- My kid is definitely
gonna have coffee and candy

01:12:49.100 --> 01:12:50.713
for sort of late night emails, and.

01:12:50.713 --> 01:12:54.270
- Yeah, your kids are lucky
that you're so disciplined.

01:12:54.270 --> 01:12:55.690
They get treat.

01:12:55.690 --> 01:12:57.743
Rod, did you wanna jump in here?

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:00.833
- Well, let's see.

01:13:00.833 --> 01:13:03.963
Maybe I'll have some fun with A,

01:13:05.410 --> 01:13:06.983
what would I bring with me?

01:13:10.920 --> 01:13:14.770
Honestly, I would bring with me

01:13:14.770 --> 01:13:19.770
a pen and a paper, and a big
note board for the students.

01:13:25.360 --> 01:13:29.923
One of the reasons I love that
passage from Derrida is that,

01:13:31.330 --> 01:13:34.940
in addition to saying that the
imagining of the institution,

01:13:34.940 --> 01:13:37.620
or the re-imagining of the
institution is immediate

01:13:37.620 --> 01:13:40.623
to what we do, it's also very simple.

01:13:41.480 --> 01:13:44.323
It means that, okay, let me start writing,

01:13:45.760 --> 01:13:49.740
or let me start crafting this course.

01:13:49.740 --> 01:13:52.003
And in that moment,

01:13:53.300 --> 01:13:57.990
a new socius, a new community,
can be brought into being.

01:13:57.990 --> 01:13:59.910
So, I mean, at the end of the day,

01:13:59.910 --> 01:14:02.050
I joke with my students sometimes,

01:14:02.050 --> 01:14:06.900
being from rural Georgia and
having very good Black teachers

01:14:09.030 --> 01:14:11.750
who were veterans of segregated teaching,

01:14:11.750 --> 01:14:12.920
that at the end of the day,

01:14:12.920 --> 01:14:14.730
I am that old Negro intellectual.

01:14:14.730 --> 01:14:17.920
You give me a pen and a paper,

01:14:17.920 --> 01:14:20.753
let me teach, let me write,
and I can do something.

01:14:22.250 --> 01:14:24.910
- Yeah, yeah, give me the material.

01:14:24.910 --> 01:14:28.200
- Just give me the material,
and let me do my job,

01:14:28.200 --> 01:14:31.360
and something's gonna happen.

01:14:31.360 --> 01:14:32.260
- Right, right.

01:14:32.260 --> 01:14:34.160
So, I mean, that's interesting because

01:14:37.290 --> 01:14:40.450
a lot of your comments as
a panel were a combination

01:14:40.450 --> 01:14:44.100
of sort of taking a certain
kind of for granted-ness

01:14:44.100 --> 01:14:45.650
of what Rod just emphasized,

01:14:45.650 --> 01:14:48.450
which is that you're
bringing to these objects,

01:14:48.450 --> 01:14:52.410
bringing to these museums,
bringing to various departments,

01:14:52.410 --> 01:14:55.470
bringing to visual
culture, and art history,

01:14:55.470 --> 01:14:56.710
a whole set of framework.

01:14:56.710 --> 01:14:58.140
So, a tradition of critique,

01:14:58.140 --> 01:15:01.630
a tradition of transformation and refusal

01:15:01.630 --> 01:15:04.110
that is part of a process.

01:15:04.110 --> 01:15:06.560
I just wanna put that on the extra.

01:15:06.560 --> 01:15:07.630
I mean, Rod was just saying,

01:15:07.630 --> 01:15:09.750
you know where I'm coming
from, just give me some paper,

01:15:09.750 --> 01:15:12.480
but the paper has to be
in the right hands, right,

01:15:12.480 --> 01:15:14.380
the paper and the pen.

01:15:14.380 --> 01:15:17.470
And so, all of you are
actually articulating this.

01:15:17.470 --> 01:15:21.780
And in that sense, I'd say
the knives and emergency packs

01:15:21.780 --> 01:15:23.810
are not literal, right,

01:15:23.810 --> 01:15:26.890
they're really the way we do what we do,

01:15:26.890 --> 01:15:29.130
not the object that we do it with.

01:15:29.130 --> 01:15:31.100
So, whether it's a knife
or an emergency pack,

01:15:31.100 --> 01:15:33.750
it doesn't actually
matter, it's really about

01:15:33.750 --> 01:15:38.003
the form of intensive critique
and refusal, to some degree.

01:15:39.430 --> 01:15:42.230
So, before we close, I
just wanna give you all

01:15:42.230 --> 01:15:43.210
an opportunity, first of all,

01:15:43.210 --> 01:15:45.200
to ask each other any questions,

01:15:45.200 --> 01:15:49.280
to talk them about things that
I unfortunately stepped over

01:15:49.280 --> 01:15:52.410
that I shouldn't have, or things
that have come to your mind

01:15:52.410 --> 01:15:55.100
that you wanna leave us with

01:15:55.100 --> 01:15:56.910
in the form of closing comments,

01:15:56.910 --> 01:16:00.340
'cause by the time we
get to you all speaking,

01:16:00.340 --> 01:16:02.453
it'll probably be a little bit past 4:30.

01:16:03.540 --> 01:16:05.160
So, I'm just gonna leave the floor open.

01:16:05.160 --> 01:16:08.590
And if you don't have anything
other than closing comments,

01:16:08.590 --> 01:16:10.300
just offer some closing comments

01:16:10.300 --> 01:16:15.300
about how we can really pursue
this kind of intervention

01:16:15.990 --> 01:16:17.663
that we want to see.

01:16:21.300 --> 01:16:23.090
- Can I make a quick comment,

01:16:23.090 --> 01:16:26.240
getting back to something
Matt said earlier,

01:16:26.240 --> 01:16:30.061
when you were talking, Matt,
about the English discipline

01:16:30.061 --> 01:16:34.290
and the fact that it's been
limited by its inability

01:16:34.290 --> 01:16:36.800
to shed stupid periodization

01:16:36.800 --> 01:16:38.450
or division on the basis of language,

01:16:38.450 --> 01:16:40.610
which I resent very deeply?

01:16:40.610 --> 01:16:42.810
So, I'm on board with you there.

01:16:42.810 --> 01:16:45.020
But you also mentioned
something very interesting,

01:16:45.020 --> 01:16:47.840
which is that medieval
studies, in particular,

01:16:47.840 --> 01:16:50.280
have become, the knowledge,

01:16:50.280 --> 01:16:51.217
the extraordinary body of knowledge

01:16:51.217 --> 01:16:55.740
that's been produced over
decades in medieval studies

01:16:55.740 --> 01:16:57.400
has suddenly become the bedrock

01:16:57.400 --> 01:17:01.540
of a lot of White supremacist
propaganda these days.

01:17:01.540 --> 01:17:02.510
Absolutely true.

01:17:02.510 --> 01:17:05.040
I just wanna point out that
we're fighting back, right,

01:17:05.040 --> 01:17:06.050
we're fighting back.

01:17:06.050 --> 01:17:09.320
And RaceB4Race, I'll keep mentioning it,

01:17:09.320 --> 01:17:12.770
was based upon the idea of
bringing together medievalists

01:17:12.770 --> 01:17:15.300
who don't yet have a very strong tradition

01:17:15.300 --> 01:17:17.260
of working with critical race theory,

01:17:17.260 --> 01:17:19.080
bringing them together
with early modernists

01:17:19.080 --> 01:17:21.130
who have been doing that since the '90s,

01:17:21.130 --> 01:17:22.340
and the next iteration

01:17:22.340 --> 01:17:24.500
of the conference at Brandeis next week

01:17:24.500 --> 01:17:26.690
is going to actually bring in classists.

01:17:26.690 --> 01:17:29.090
So, we're going back further and further

01:17:29.090 --> 01:17:30.600
dealing with RaceB4Race.

01:17:30.600 --> 01:17:33.930
But the idea is that precisely
we can not let that happen.

01:17:33.930 --> 01:17:37.750
We can not let all the cultural artifacts

01:17:37.750 --> 01:17:40.190
produced during those time periods

01:17:40.190 --> 01:17:43.120
become fodder for that nonsense.

01:17:43.120 --> 01:17:45.240
And so, that, I think,

01:17:45.240 --> 01:17:47.910
is what actually makes
our work meaningful.

01:17:47.910 --> 01:17:49.770
- Yeah. Let me say two quick things.

01:17:49.770 --> 01:17:51.690
Can you repeat the Brandeis conference

01:17:51.690 --> 01:17:54.150
because there were people
asking about it in the chat

01:17:54.150 --> 01:17:55.550
and I didn't wanna interrupt?

01:17:55.550 --> 01:17:58.283
So, or if you could put it in the chat.

01:17:59.870 --> 01:18:02.320
- I think I dropped the link
earlier, but I will redo it,

01:18:02.320 --> 01:18:04.020
I will drop the link in the chat again.

01:18:04.020 --> 01:18:07.860
It's RaceB4Race at Brandeis University.

01:18:07.860 --> 01:18:09.380
It starts next week.

01:18:09.380 --> 01:18:11.220
- Great, great. Yeah.

01:18:11.220 --> 01:18:12.760
And just to really comment

01:18:12.760 --> 01:18:15.000
on the so important observation you made

01:18:15.000 --> 01:18:18.310
that we think of social
justice and racial intervention

01:18:18.310 --> 01:18:20.650
being the beginning of a conversation

01:18:20.650 --> 01:18:23.850
about racial intervention,
when in fact, the disciplines

01:18:23.850 --> 01:18:26.860
as colonial forms of ideology formation

01:18:26.860 --> 01:18:29.954
were all actually the first
racial interventionists, right,

01:18:29.954 --> 01:18:31.940
and they're coming back strong.

01:18:31.940 --> 01:18:33.240
But anyway, I'll leave that alone.

01:18:33.240 --> 01:18:35.000
But I just thought that
was super important.

01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:38.680
Anybody else in closing
or any other thoughts?

01:18:38.680 --> 01:18:41.160
- I was just gonna say
to Noemie's comments,

01:18:41.160 --> 01:18:44.180
I remember how thrilling it was

01:18:44.180 --> 01:18:48.010
when I was an undergraduate
at Howard in 1990

01:18:48.010 --> 01:18:51.130
to discover that folks like Martin Bernal

01:18:51.130 --> 01:18:55.200
were intervening in the field of classics.

01:18:55.200 --> 01:18:58.950
And then later on, when
I was a grad student

01:18:58.950 --> 01:19:01.540
at UC San Diego,

01:19:01.540 --> 01:19:02.700
taking a course,

01:19:02.700 --> 01:19:05.300
a feminist theory
course, with Paige DuBois

01:19:07.720 --> 01:19:12.720
and her interventions
via Marxism and feminism,

01:19:12.930 --> 01:19:16.670
and post-colonial studies, and
psychoanalysis in classics,

01:19:16.670 --> 01:19:20.610
and just seeing the ways in which,

01:19:20.610 --> 01:19:24.670
both at Howard and at UCSD's
literature department,

01:19:24.670 --> 01:19:27.930
that those interventions became the sites

01:19:27.930 --> 01:19:32.420
for the introduction of
new feminist subjects,

01:19:32.420 --> 01:19:35.170
new Black subjects in those fields,

01:19:35.170 --> 01:19:38.970
it was really, really an
exciting thing to witness,

01:19:38.970 --> 01:19:40.073
and also to repeat.

01:19:47.540 --> 01:19:49.230
- I mean, I would wanna return

01:19:49.230 --> 01:19:52.320
to one of the things I
said in my opening remarks,

01:19:52.320 --> 01:19:57.320
which is that, in a way,
I really do feel enveloped

01:19:57.390 --> 01:20:00.540
in such an abundance of interesting work,

01:20:00.540 --> 01:20:05.540
interesting, relevant, just
and righteous work on race

01:20:05.920 --> 01:20:08.020
that falls within the humanities,

01:20:08.020 --> 01:20:10.980
in a way that I didn't feel
when I was a graduate student,

01:20:10.980 --> 01:20:15.810
that the sheer volume of
material that lands on my desk

01:20:15.810 --> 01:20:17.490
or arrives on my computer screen,

01:20:17.490 --> 01:20:21.263
or shows up on my television,
is just extraordinary.

01:20:24.090 --> 01:20:28.670
This is worth fighting for,
and it's worth trying to center

01:20:28.670 --> 01:20:30.630
to take all of that material,
worth trying to center

01:20:30.630 --> 01:20:34.430
in these medieval
institutions we inhabit here.

01:20:34.430 --> 01:20:37.080
So, I want us to sort
of think about the ways

01:20:37.080 --> 01:20:42.080
in which we can bring all
of that joyful abundance

01:20:42.140 --> 01:20:44.100
and put it at the center of these places,

01:20:44.100 --> 01:20:45.330
again, even if that means

01:20:45.330 --> 01:20:47.393
breaking them a little bit, or a lot.

01:20:48.350 --> 01:20:51.570
- Yeah, yeah, excellent. Thank you.

01:20:51.570 --> 01:20:53.173
Teresa, or Marci, or,

01:20:54.503 --> 01:20:55.813
any closing thoughts?

01:21:00.690 --> 01:21:03.010
- Yeah, I guess, maybe just that

01:21:07.540 --> 01:21:10.890
every single time I return to

01:21:12.843 --> 01:21:14.837
one of the works of art that

01:21:19.810 --> 01:21:21.720
I've been really spent

01:21:23.170 --> 01:21:25.513
my time at Stanford looking at,

01:21:28.080 --> 01:21:30.080
it always exceeds

01:21:30.950 --> 01:21:33.500
the capacity of any single
discourse to capture it.

01:21:39.344 --> 01:21:41.011
- All right. Teresa.

01:21:42.570 --> 01:21:44.000
- Well, I just wanna say thank you

01:21:44.000 --> 01:21:46.430
to everyone for having me here.

01:21:46.430 --> 01:21:47.630
I wanna give a shout out

01:21:47.630 --> 01:21:50.980
to my colleague at U
Chicago, Sarah Johnson.

01:21:50.980 --> 01:21:53.540
We were actually awarded
some Mellon funding

01:21:53.540 --> 01:21:55.390
for the next academic year.

01:21:55.390 --> 01:21:58.200
So, we're gonna be starting this project

01:21:58.200 --> 01:21:59.610
called "Tracing Relations:

01:21:59.610 --> 01:22:01.870
Blackness and Indigeneity
in the Americas".

01:22:01.870 --> 01:22:03.703
And this is inspired by,

01:22:05.603 --> 01:22:07.430
she's in English and Black Studies,

01:22:07.430 --> 01:22:09.570
and I'm coming from
Anthropology and Native Studies,

01:22:09.570 --> 01:22:12.070
and I think both of us
have come to the space

01:22:12.070 --> 01:22:14.077
of vulnerability together.

01:22:14.077 --> 01:22:17.280
There's so much that I have yet to learn

01:22:17.280 --> 01:22:19.040
in reading of Black studies

01:22:19.040 --> 01:22:21.190
as someone who's mostly been
reading in Native studies,

01:22:21.190 --> 01:22:23.353
and her, vice versa.

01:22:24.840 --> 01:22:28.190
So, for anyone from U
Chicago who's watching,

01:22:28.190 --> 01:22:29.260
just be on the lookout

01:22:29.260 --> 01:22:31.490
'cause we're gonna be
having reading groups

01:22:31.490 --> 01:22:33.740
and film screenings next year,

01:22:33.740 --> 01:22:36.670
convened around this orienting
theme of relationality

01:22:36.670 --> 01:22:38.699
that we find really important

01:22:38.699 --> 01:22:43.163
to both of those
disciplines and discourses.

01:22:44.300 --> 01:22:46.580
But I feel like some of the discussions

01:22:46.580 --> 01:22:47.510
we've been having today

01:22:47.510 --> 01:22:49.240
has actually given me a lot of new ideas

01:22:49.240 --> 01:22:51.560
for how we can move forward on that.

01:22:51.560 --> 01:22:53.380
We do plan to make a website for this,

01:22:53.380 --> 01:22:55.720
and maybe we'll have
some virtual component

01:22:55.720 --> 01:22:59.670
that we can continue with
the same group of people,

01:22:59.670 --> 01:23:00.700
but we don't have that yet.

01:23:00.700 --> 01:23:03.570
So, I just wanna say thank you
for giving me the opportunity

01:23:03.570 --> 01:23:06.020
to think further on this project.

01:23:06.020 --> 01:23:06.853
- Yes, yes, indeed.

01:23:06.853 --> 01:23:10.090
And even I feel the same way,
and I'm sure my Director,

01:23:10.090 --> 01:23:13.750
colleagues, Jennifer,
and O'Riley, and Steve,

01:23:13.750 --> 01:23:17.710
and I'm I missing anybody,
no, it's four of us,

01:23:17.710 --> 01:23:19.753
I always have to count
one, two, three, four.

01:23:20.909 --> 01:23:23.640
I've been super inspired by
listening to all of you here,

01:23:23.640 --> 01:23:28.030
really thinks in such rich
ways about the problem,

01:23:28.030 --> 01:23:29.834
the solutions, the impossibilities,

01:23:29.834 --> 01:23:33.520
the places to both hide
out and to be visible,

01:23:33.520 --> 01:23:37.340
and the role of just
such creative thinking,

01:23:37.340 --> 01:23:39.330
which, I think, has
been so important here.

01:23:39.330 --> 01:23:42.380
So, I'm really inspired,
and I'm hoping our grant

01:23:42.380 --> 01:23:45.630
will benefit from and take
out a number of the things

01:23:45.630 --> 01:23:46.730
that we've been doing here.

01:23:46.730 --> 01:23:48.367
So, thank you so much, all of you,

01:23:48.367 --> 01:23:50.970
for being a part of this inaugural,

01:23:50.970 --> 01:23:52.740
what we hope may be an annual conference

01:23:52.740 --> 01:23:53.573
on racial reckoning,

01:23:53.573 --> 01:23:56.330
'cause we wanna keep opening up the space.

01:23:56.330 --> 01:23:58.550
Let us know what you're
working on, stay in touch.

01:23:58.550 --> 01:24:00.810
And thank those of you who
showed up in the audience

01:24:00.810 --> 01:24:01.918
and asked great questions,

01:24:01.918 --> 01:24:04.380
and hung out with us this afternoon.

01:24:04.380 --> 01:24:05.550
And we will see you again.

01:24:05.550 --> 01:24:09.010
There's an event tomorrow
at the same time.

01:24:09.010 --> 01:24:11.010
I believe it's the same link for sign up,

01:24:11.010 --> 01:24:14.160
but I'm sure you can find
that how you found this,

01:24:14.160 --> 01:24:15.990
and maybe I'll put it in the chat.

01:24:15.990 --> 01:24:17.940
So, thank you all very much for coming,

01:24:17.940 --> 01:24:19.730
and thanks again to the fine panelists

01:24:19.730 --> 01:24:22.250
for being really terrific.

01:24:22.250 --> 01:24:24.250
Thanks very much, everybody. Good night.

